tember 2026</p><h1 data-layer="fact">Payrolls rose by an estimated 29,000 in September, a change too small for the survey to tell from none</h1><p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">Economists surveyed by Reuters expected payrolls to rise by 90,000. The Bureau of Labor Statistics said payrolls and the 4.2 percent unemployment rate both changed little. July and August together are 60,000 lower than previously reported.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-20" data-record-href="/records/reuters-september-jobs-preview/" data-passage="Nonfarm payrolls likely increased by 90,000 last month" aria-label="Source 20: read the evidence">20</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="Both nonfarm payroll employment (+29,000) and the unemployment rate (4.2 percent) changed little in September" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="employment in July and August combined is 60,000 lower than previously reported">1</a></sup></p><div class="mode-switcher" role="radiogroup" aria-label="Reading mode"><a class="mode-btn active" href="?mode=story" data-mode="story" role="radio" aria-checked="true">The story</a><a class="mode-btn" href="?mode=facts" data-mode="facts" role="radio" aria-checked="false">Just the facts</a><a class="mode-btn" href="?mode=work" data-mode="work" role="radio" aria-checked="false">Show the work</a></div><script type="module">document.addEventListener(`DOMContentLoaded`,()=>{let e=document.getElementById(`story-root`),t=document.querySelectorAll(`.mode-btn`);if(!e||!t.length)return;let n=new URLSearchParams(window.location.search),r=[`story`,`facts`,`work`].includes(n.get(`mode`))?n.get(`mode`):`story`;e.classList.add(`mode-${r}`);let i=e.querySelector(`[data-record-ledger]`);i&&(i.open=r===`work`),t.forEach(e=>{let t=e.getAttribute(`data-mode`)===r;e.classList.toggle(`active`,t),e.setAttribute(`aria-checked`,t?`true`:`false`),e.setAttribute(`tabindex`,t?`0`:`-1`)}),t.forEach((n,r)=>{n.addEventListener(`keydown`,e=>{let n=e.key===`ArrowRight`||e.key===`ArrowDown`?1:e.key===`ArrowLeft`||e.key===`ArrowUp`?-1:0;if(!n&&e.key!==`Home`&&e.key!==`End`&&e.key!==` `)return;e.preventDefault();let i=e.key===`Home`?t[0]:e.key===`End`?t[t.length-1]:t[(r+n+t.length)%t.length];i.click(),i.focus()}),n.addEventListener(`click`,r=>{r.preventDefault();let a=[...e.querySelectorAll(`section[id], aside[id]`)],o=a.find(e=>`#`+e.id===location.hash&&e.getBoundingClientRect().top>=0&&e.getBoundingClientRect().top<innerHeight)??a.filter(e=>e.getBoundingClientRect().height&&e.getBoundingClientRect().top<=160).at(-1),s=n.getAttribute(`data-mode`);i&&(i.open=s===`work`),e.className=e.className.replace(/mode-\w+/g,``).trim()+` mode-${s}`,t.forEach(e=>{let t=e===n;e.classList.toggle(`active`,t),e.setAttribute(`aria-checked`,t?`true`:`false`),e.setAttribute(`tabindex`,t?`0`:`-1`)});let c=new URL(window.location.href);if(c.searchParams.set(`mode`,s),o){let e=a.indexOf(o),t=o.getBoundingClientRect().height?o:[...a.slice(0,e).reverse(),...a.slice(e+1)].find(e=>e.getBoundingClientRect().height);t&&(requestAnimationFrame(()=>t.scrollIntoView({block:`start`})),c.hash=t.id)}history.replaceState(null,``,c.toString()),document.dispatchEvent(new Event(`reading-mode-change`))})})});</script><nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story" data-layer="fact"><details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><ol><li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li><li><a href="#the-catch">The catch</a></li><li><a href="#where-this-sits">Where this sits</a></li><li><a href="#unknowns">What we do not know yet</a></li><li><a href="#chronology">Chronology</a></li><li><a href="#records">The records</a></li></ol></details></nav><div class="kpi-strip" data-layer="fact"><div class="kpi"><span class="kpi-value">+29,000<small></small></span><span class="kpi-label">payrolls in September</span></div><div class="kpi"><span class="kpi-value">4.2<small> %</small></span><span class="kpi-label">unemployment rate</span></div><div class="kpi"><span class="kpi-value">60,000<small> lower</small></span><span class="kpi-label">July and August, revised</span></div></div><section id="what-happened"><h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2><p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="1">The Bureau of Labor Statistics reported on Friday, October 2, 2026, that payroll employment rose by 29,000 in September and the unemployment rate was 4.2 percent, and said both changed little.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="8:30 a.m. (ET) Friday, October 2, 2026" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="Both nonfarm payroll employment (+29,000) and the unemployment rate (4.2 percent) changed little in September">1</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="1">Private payrolls rose by 46,000 and government payrolls fell by 17,000.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="Total private | 68 | 28 | 89 | 46" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="Government | 8 | -38 | 44 | -17">1</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="1">The bureau also revised the two prior months down by 60,000 together: August from a gain of 162,000 to 133,000, and July from a gain of 21,000 to a loss of 10,000.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="The change in total nonfarm payroll employment for July was revised down by 31,000, from +21,000 to -10,000, and the change for August was revised down by 29,000, from +162,000 to +133,000." aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="employment in July and August combined is 60,000 lower than previously reported">1</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="4">The establishment survey counts jobs on employer payrolls. The household survey counts people, and individuals are counted only once. A jobs number and a people number can move differently in the same month. The payroll count comes from the first survey, and the unemployment rate from the second.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="The household survey measures labor force status, including unemployment, by demographic characteristics. The establishment survey measures nonfarm employment, hours, and earnings by industry." aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="individuals are counted only once">1</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="2">The Federal Reserve's two goals are maximum employment and price stability.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-6" data-record-href="/records/fomc-longer-run-goals-2025/" data-passage="maximum employment and price stability" aria-label="Source 6: read the evidence">6</a></sup> Its primary means of adjusting the stance of monetary policy is through changes in the target range for the federal funds rate.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-6" data-record-href="/records/fomc-longer-run-goals-2025/" data-passage="primary means of adjusting the stance of monetary policy is through changes in the target range for the federal funds rate" aria-label="Source 6: read the evidence">6</a></sup> On September 16 it raised that range by 1/4 percentage point<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-5" data-record-href="/records/fomc-statement-2026-09-16/" data-passage="The Committee decided to raise the target range for the federal funds rate by 1/4 percentage point to 3-3/4 to 4 percent" aria-label="Source 5: read the evidence">5</a></sup> in support of those goals.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-5" data-record-href="/records/fomc-statement-2026-09-16/" data-passage="in support of the Federal Reserve's dual mandate" aria-label="Source 5: read the evidence">5</a></sup> This report is the employment reading before that committee meets on October 27-28; the next one, for October, is scheduled for November 6.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-9" data-record-href="/records/fomc-calendar-2026-09-16/" data-passage="October 27-28" aria-label="Source 9: read the evidence">9</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="The Employment Situation for October 2026 is scheduled to be published on Friday, November 6, 2026" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup> On prices, the personal consumption expenditures price index for August, whose annual change is how the committee measures inflation,<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-6" data-record-href="/records/fomc-longer-run-goals-2025/" data-passage="as measured by the annual change in the price index for personal consumption expenditures" aria-label="Source 6: read the evidence">6</a></sup> was up 3.4 percent from a year earlier.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-7" data-record-href="/records/bea-pce-2026-08/" data-passage="From the same month one year ago, the PCE price index for August increased 3.4 percent." aria-label="Source 7: read the evidence">7</a></sup> This report put average hourly earnings up 3.0 percent over the past 12 months.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="Over the past 12 months, average hourly earnings have increased by 3.0 percent" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup></p><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="bls-empsit-2026-09"><p>The Bureau of Labor Statistics reported on Friday, October 2, 2026, that payroll employment rose by 29,000 in September and the unemployment rate was 4.2 percent, and said both changed little. The release was embargoed until 8:30 a.m. Eastern that morning; the calendar schedules the same time. The unemployment rate has stayed between 4.1 percent and 4.3 percent since March, and 7.1 million people were unemployed.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="8:30 a.m. (ET) Friday, October 2, 2026" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="Both the unemployment rate, at 4.2 percent, and the number of unemployed people, at 7.1 million, changed little in September">1</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-3" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-schedule-2026-10-03/" data-passage="September 2026 | Oct. 02, 2026 | 08:30 AM" aria-label="Source 3: read the evidence">3</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="bls-empsit-2026-09"><p>July was revised down by 31,000, from a gain of 21,000 to a loss of 10,000. August was revised down by 29,000, from a gain of 162,000 to a gain of 133,000. The August release had reported a gain of 162,000. The two months together are 60,000 lower than previously reported.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="employment in July and August combined is 60,000 lower than previously reported" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-4" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-08/" data-passage="Total nonfarm payroll employment increased by 162,000 in August, and the unemployment rate was unchanged at 4.1 percent" aria-label="Source 4: read the evidence">4</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div><details class="receipt" data-layer="proof"><summary>July and August, as the September release states them</summary><figure class="data-table-wrap"><table class="data-table"><caption>Changes in thousands of jobs, from the September release.</caption><thead><tr><th class>Month</th><th class>Previously reported</th><th class>September release</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class>July</td><td class>+21,000</td><td class>-10,000</td></tr><tr><td class>August</td><td class>+162,000</td><td class>+133,000</td></tr><tr><td class>Two months combined</td><td class></td><td class>60,000 lower</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></details><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="bls-empsit-2026-09"><p>Employment in every major industry changed little. Health care added 17,000, slower than its average monthly gain of 33,000 over the prior 12 months. Ambulatory care added 13,000 and hospitals 12,000. Nursing and residential care lost 9,000. Construction added 11,000. Manufacturing added 9,000 and is up by 72,000 since a recent low in December 2025. Financial activities lost 7,000 and are down 129,000 since a peak in May 2025, most of that in insurance carriers and related activities.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="Health care employment continued its upward trend in September (+17,000)" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="Employment in all major industries changed little over the month&#10;nursing and residential care facilities lost jobs (-9,000)&#10;Manufacturing employment was little changed in September (+9,000) but is up by 72,000 since a recent low in December 2025.&#10;Employment in financial activities is down by 129,000 since a recent peak in May 2025&#10;insurance carriers and related activities (-90,000).">1</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="bls-empsit-2026-09"><p>The one-month diffusion index for 250 private industries was 49.0 in September after 57.6 in August. The release counts it as the percent of industries with employment increasing plus one-half of the industries with unchanged employment, and says 50 percent is an equal balance between industries adding and cutting jobs.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="Figures are the percent of industries with employment increasing plus one-half of the industries with unchanged employment" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup> Private payrolls rose by 46,000 and government payrolls fell by 17,000. Information fell by 10,000. Temporary help services fell by 10.9 thousand.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="Total private (250 industries) | 50.4 | 54.4 | 57.6 | 49.0" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="50 percent indicates an equal balance between industries with increasing and decreasing employment&#10;Total private | 68 | 28 | 89 | 46&#10;Government | 8 | -38 | 44 | -17&#10;Information | -1 | 4 | -18 | -10&#10;Temporary help services | -11.7 | -10.0 | 0.2 | -10.9">1</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="bls-empsit-2026-09"><p>Average hourly earnings edged up 5 cents, or 0.1 percent, to $37.81. Over the year they are up 3.0 percent. The average workweek stayed at 34.4 hours. A change of about 122,000 jobs is what the bureau calls statistically significant in the employer survey, so the 29,000 gain does not clear that bar. The household survey's bar is about 650,000.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="average hourly earnings for all employees on private nonfarm payrolls edged up by 5 cents, or 0.1 percent, to $37.81" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="An over-the-month employment change of about 122,000 is statistically significant in the establishment survey">1</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="ap-september-jobs"><p>The Associated Press called that yearly wage gain &ldquo;the smallest year-over-year gain since May 2021&rdquo;.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-23" data-record-href="/records/ap-september-jobs/" data-passage="the smallest year-over-year gain since May 2021" aria-label="Source 23: read the evidence">23</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-checked">checked</span></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="cnbc-september-jobs"><p>CNBC wrote that the average workweek was unchanged at 34.6 hours. CNBC's 34.6 hours is not the release's figure. The release says the workweek remained at 34.4 hours.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-18" data-record-href="/records/cnbc-september-jobs/" data-passage="The average work week was unchanged at 34.6 hours" aria-label="Source 18: read the evidence">18</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="remained at 34.4 hours in September" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-checked">checked</span></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="bls-empsit-2026-09"><p>The unemployment rate for Black workers rose to 7.0 percent. The bureau said that rate increased, and that the rates for adult men (3.9 percent), adult women (3.6 percent), teenagers (14.5 percent), and White (3.6), Asian (2.9), and Hispanic (4.7) workers showed little change. The labor force participation rate was 61.8 percent and the employment-population ratio was 59.2 percent. The labor force rose by 485,000 and household employment by 406,000.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="the unemployment rate for people who are Black (7.0 percent) increased in September" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="Both the labor force participation rate, at 61.8 percent, and the employment-population ratio, at 59.2 percent, changed little in September">1</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="bls-empsit-2026-09"><p>Job losers and people who completed temporary jobs fell by 45,000. Job leavers fell by 173,000. Reentrants rose by 152,000 and new entrants rose by 116,000. The summary table counts those groups in thousands.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="Job losers and people who completed temporary jobs | 3,524 | 3,309 | 3,245 | 3,200 | -45" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="Job leavers | 860 | 793 | 914 | 741 | -173&#10;Reentrants | 2,336 | 2,123 | 2,137 | 2,289 | 152&#10;New entrants | 813 | 761 | 702 | 818 | 116">1</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="bls-empsit-2026-09"><p>The long-term unemployed, out of work 27 weeks or more, were essentially unchanged at 1.9 million and were 27.1 percent of unemployed people. People employed part time for economic reasons were little changed at 4.5 million. They would have preferred full-time employment but were working part time because their hours had been reduced or they were unable to find full-time jobs.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="These individuals would have preferred full-time employment but were working part time because their hours had been reduced or they were unable to find full-time jobs." aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup> People marginally attached to the labor force fell by 236,000 to 1.5 million. Discouraged workers were little changed at 414,000.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="The number of long-term unemployed (those jobless for 27 weeks or more) was essentially unchanged at 1.9 million in September" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="The number of people employed part time for economic reasons changed little at 4.5 million in September">1</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="bls-empsit-2026-09"><p>The broader underemployment rate, U-6, was 7.6 percent in September, after 7.7 percent in August.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="U-6 Total unemployed, plus all people marginally attached to the labor force, plus total employed part time for economic reasons, as a percent of the civilian labor force plus all people marginally attached to the labor force | 7.7 | 7.8 | 7.3 | 8.1 | 8.1 | 7.9 | 7.9 | 7.7 | 7.6" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-12" data-record-href="/records/fred-u6-2026-10-03/" data-passage="2026-09-01,7.6" aria-label="Source 12: read the evidence">12</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="bls-empsit-t09-2026-09"><p>The household survey counted 8,986,000 people working more than one job in September, 5.5 percent of everyone employed, against 8,805,000 in August. The table counts those people in thousands.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-2" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-t09-2026-09/" data-passage="Total multiple jobholders | 8,794 | 8,505 | 9,000 | 8,810 | 8,428 | 8,554 | 8,693 | 8,805 | 8,986" aria-label="Source 2: read the evidence">2</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="fred-prime-age-2026-10-03"><p>Prime-age participation was 83.7 percent in September, up from 83.4 percent in August.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-13" data-record-href="/records/fred-prime-age-2026-10-03/" data-passage="2026-08-01,83.4" aria-label="Source 13: read the evidence" data-passages="2026-09-01,83.7">13</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-official">official data</span></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="fred-ahe-2026-10-03"><p>On the hourly-earnings series, September's $37.81 is 3.02 percent above September 2025. The release rounds that change to 3.0 percent.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-14" data-record-href="/records/fred-ahe-2026-10-03/" data-passage="2026-09-01,37.81" aria-label="Source 14: read the evidence">14</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="Over the past 12 months, average hourly earnings have increased by 3.0 percent" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-official">official data</span></div></section><section id="the-catch"><h2 data-layer="fact">The catch</h2><p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="3">USA Today headlined the release &ldquo;September jobs report shows slowdown in hiring, unemployment ticks up&rdquo;.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-25" data-record-href="/records/usatoday-september-jobs/" data-passage="September jobs report shows slowdown in hiring, unemployment ticks up" aria-label="Source 25: read the evidence">25</a></sup> The bureau's table shows the rounded unemployment rate up from 4.1 to 4.2, and its summary says payrolls and that rate both &ldquo;changed little&rdquo;, which describes the size of the move.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="Unemployment rate | 4.4 | 4.1 | 4.1 | 4.2 | 0.1" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="Both nonfarm payroll employment (+29,000) and the unemployment rate (4.2 percent) changed little in September">1</a></sup> The 29,000 is a net change in payroll jobs, not a count of hires, and it is under about 122,000, the monthly change the bureau says is statistically significant.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="An over-the-month employment change of about 122,000 is statistically significant in the establishment survey" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="Total nonfarm payroll employment changed little in September (+29,000)">1</a></sup> Hires, &ldquo;all additions to the payroll&rdquo; during the month, are counted in a separate survey, and September's count is scheduled for November 3.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-8" data-record-href="/records/bls-jolts-2026-08/" data-passage="all additions to the payroll" aria-label="Source 8: read the evidence" data-passages="The Job Openings and Labor Turnover news release for September 2026 is scheduled to be published on Tuesday, November 3, 2026, at 10:00 a.m. (ET).">8</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">By the household survey's estimates, employment rose by 406,000, which is under the 650,000 that survey needs before a change is statistically significant.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="Employed | 163,656 | 162,177 | 162,746 | 163,152 | 406" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="An over-the-month employment change of about 122,000 is statistically significant in the establishment survey, while the threshold for a statistically significant change in the household survey is about 650,000.">1</a></sup> The labor force, the employed plus the unemployed, rose by 485,000.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="Civilian labor force | 171,261 | 169,094 | 169,777 | 170,262 | 485" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="The civilian labor force is the sum of the employed and unemployed">1</a></sup> The unemployment rate is the number unemployed as a percent of the labor force. The labor force grew by more than employment did, so the count of unemployed rose by 78,000 and the rate went from 4.1 to 4.2 percent.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="The unemployment rate is the number unemployed as a percent of the labor force" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="Unemployed | 7,605 | 6,916 | 7,031 | 7,109 | 78&#10;Unemployment rate | 4.4 | 4.1 | 4.1 | 4.2 | 0.1">1</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">Most of that tenth is rounding. Before rounding, the rate &ldquo;increased slightly from 4.141% to 4.175%&rdquo;, the St. Louis Fed wrote on October 2, a rise of about 0.03 point.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-17" data-record-href="/records/stlouisfed-unrate-2026-10/" data-passage="More precise data show the rate increased slightly from 4.141% to 4.175%." aria-label="Source 17: read the evidence" data-passages="St. Louis Fed On the Economy, Oct. 2, 2026.">17</a></sup> The rate has stayed between 4.1 percent and 4.3 percent since March.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="The unemployment rate has remained in a narrow range of 4.1 percent to 4.3 percent since March." aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">The bureau named one group whose rate increased: Black workers, from 6.0 percent in August to 7.0 percent in September.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="the unemployment rate for people who are Black (7.0 percent) increased" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="Black or African American | 7.6 | 6.3 | 6.0 | 7.0 | 1.0&#10;Unemployment rate | 4.4 | 4.1 | 4.1 | 4.2 | 0.1">1</a></sup> A year earlier it was 7.6 percent.</p></section><section id="where-this-sits"><h2 data-layer="fact">Where this sits</h2><figure class="payroll-chart" data-layer="fact" id="payroll-chart"><figcaption><strong>Jobs gained and lost each month</strong><span>October 2025 to September 2026 · Seasonally adjusted</span></figcaption><svg viewBox="0 0 720 240" role="img" aria-label="Monthly payroll change. September 2026: +29,000 jobs. October 2025 to September 2026. Positive bars show gains; negative bars show losses."><g><line x1="48" x2="708" y1="169.33333333333331" y2="169.33333333333331" class="payroll-grid"></line><text x="40" y="173.33333333333331" text-anchor="end">-100k</text></g><g><line x1="48" x2="708" y1="122.66666666666667" y2="122.66666666666667" class="payroll-grid payroll-zero"></line><text x="40" y="126.66666666666667" text-anchor="end">0k</text></g><g><line x1="48" x2="708" y1="76" y2="76" class="payroll-grid"></line><text x="40" y="80" text-anchor="end">+100k</text></g><g><line x1="48" x2="708" y1="29.333333333333332" y2="29.333333333333332" class="payroll-grid"></line><text x="40" y="33.33333333333333" text-anchor="end">+200k</text></g><g><rect x="52" y="122.66666666666667" width="47" height="65.33333333333333" class="payroll-bar payroll-loss"><title>October 2025: -140,000 jobs</title></rect><text x="75.5" y="228" text-anchor="middle">Oct 25</text></g><g><rect x="107" y="103.53333333333333" width="47" height="19.13333333333334" class="payroll-bar"><title>November 2025: +41,000 jobs</title></rect></g><g><rect x="162" y="122.66666666666667" width="47" height="7.933333333333323" class="payroll-bar payroll-loss"><title>December 2025: -17,000 jobs</title></rect></g><g><rect x="217" y="48" width="47" height="74.66666666666667" class="payroll-bar"><title>January 2026: +160,000 jobs</title></rect></g><g><rect x="272" y="122.66666666666667" width="47" height="72.8" class="payroll-bar payroll-loss"><title>February 2026: -156,000 jobs</title></rect></g><g><rect x="327" y="22.8" width="47" height="99.86666666666667" class="payroll-bar"><title>March 2026: +214,000 jobs</title></rect></g><g><rect x="382" y="53.6" width="47" height="69.06666666666666" class="payroll-bar"><title>April 2026: +148,000 jobs</title></rect></g><g><rect x="437" y="93.26666666666667" width="47" height="29.400000000000006" class="payroll-bar"><title>May 2026: +63,000 jobs</title></rect><text x="460.5" y="228" text-anchor="middle">May 26</text></g><g><rect x="492" y="108.2" width="47" height="14.466666666666669" class="payroll-bar"><title>June 2026: +31,000 jobs</title></rect></g><g><rect x="547" y="122.666666666