2026-07-01,158882" aria-label="Check July 2026: -10,000 jobs">-10,000</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">August 2026</th><td><a href="/records/fred-payems-2026-10-03/" data-passage="2026-07-01,158882
2026-08-01,159015" aria-label="Check August 2026: +133,000 jobs">+133,000</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">September 2026</th><td><a href="/records/fred-payems-2026-10-03/" data-passage="2026-08-01,159015
2026-09-01,159044" aria-label="Check September 2026: +29,000 jobs">+29,000</a></td></tr></tbody></table></details></figure><p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="5">September's 29,000 followed August's revised 133,000.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="the change for August was revised down by 29,000, from +162,000 to +133,000" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup> The bureau's 45,000 a month is its average over the prior 12 months, and September is lower.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="following an average monthly gain of 45,000 over the prior 12 months" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup> The Associated Press's 68,000 a month is 2026 so far,<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-23" data-record-href="/records/ap-september-jobs/" data-passage="average 68,000 jobs a month so far this year" aria-label="Source 23: read the evidence">23</a></sup> and its 9,700 is 2025.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-23" data-record-href="/records/ap-september-jobs/" data-passage="9,700 average new jobs created every month in 2025" aria-label="Source 23: read the evidence">23</a></sup> The three averages cover different months, which is why they are not the same pace.</p><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="bls-empsit-2026-09"><p>September's 29,000 followed an average monthly gain of 45,000 over the prior 12 months, the figure the release states. Its three-month average line puts July through September at 51,000.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="following an average monthly gain of 45,000 over the prior 12 months" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="Total nonfarm | 23 | 28 | 51 | 51">1</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="fred-payems-2026-10-03"><p>On the current payroll series, the twelve monthly changes through August average 45,250 jobs. 4 of the last twelve months were declines. The nine months of 2026 average 68,000 jobs. The series prints 159044 for September and 159015 for August.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-10" data-record-href="/records/fred-payems-2026-10-03/" data-passage="2026-09-01,159044" aria-label="Source 10: read the evidence" data-passages="2026-08-01,159015">10</a></sup> Table B-1 counts employees on nonfarm payrolls in thousands and prints 159,015 and 159,044. The difference of 29 in that table is the release's change of 29,000.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="Table B-1. Employees on nonfarm payrolls by industry sector and selected industry detail" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="[In thousands]&#10;Total nonfarm | 158,729 | 158,709 | 158,905 | 159,238 | 158,548 | 158,882 | 159,015 | 159,044 | 29&#10;Both nonfarm payroll employment (+29,000) and the unemployment rate (4.2 percent) changed little in September">1</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-computed">computed</span></div><details class="receipt" data-layer="proof"><summary>How those averages were counted</summary><p>Each month is that month's payroll total minus the month before, from the series saved October 3. The twelve months before September, September 2025 through August 2026, sum to 543 thousand and divide by 12 to 45,250. The release states that average as 45,000. The last twelve months include four declines: October 2025, December 2025, February 2026, and July 2026. The 2026 average is January through September, 612 thousand divided by 9.</p></details><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="cnbc-september-jobs"><p>CNBC reported that economists surveyed by Dow Jones had looked for job growth of 84,000 and an unemployment rate of 4.1 percent. Reuters, writing before the release, said its survey of economists expected 90,000 jobs and a 4.1 percent rate for a third month. Estimates ranged from as low as 35,000 to as high as 180,000. CBS reported a FactSet forecast of 90,000. The Associated Press said economists had expected about 90,000.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-18" data-record-href="/records/cnbc-september-jobs/" data-passage="Economists surveyed by Dow Jones had been looking for job growth of 84,000 and an unemployment rate of 4.1%" aria-label="Source 18: read the evidence">18</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-20" data-record-href="/records/reuters-september-jobs-preview/" data-passage="Nonfarm payrolls likely increased by 90,000 last month" aria-label="Source 20: read the evidence" data-passages="Estimates ranged from as low as 35,000 to as high as 180,000.">20</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-22" data-record-href="/records/cbs-september-jobs/" data-passage="Economists had forecast the economy would add 90,000 new jobs last month, according to financial data firm FactSet" aria-label="Source 22: read the evidence">22</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-23" data-record-href="/records/ap-september-jobs/" data-passage="Economists had expected September payrolls to come in around 90,000" aria-label="Source 23: read the evidence">23</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div></section><section id="unknowns"><h2 data-layer="fact">What we do not know yet</h2><p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="6">The payroll figures for August and September are preliminary: the bureau says they rest on incomplete returns from the employers it surveys.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="estimates for the most recent 2 months are based on incomplete returns; for this reason, these estimates are labeled preliminary" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup> The release states no cause for the 29,000 change. Whether hiring slowed is not known yet: hires are counted separately, August's hires were 5.2 million, and September's are scheduled for November 3.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-8" data-record-href="/records/bls-jolts-2026-08/" data-passage="Hires changed little at 5.2 million" aria-label="Source 8: read the evidence" data-passages="The Job Openings and Labor Turnover news release for September 2026 is scheduled to be published on Tuesday, November 3, 2026, at 10:00 a.m. (ET).">8</a></sup></p></section><section id="chronology"><h2 data-layer="fact">Chronology</h2><div class="decision-timeline"><div class="dt-head"><span class="dt-date">Date</span><span class="dt-body">Decision</span></div><div class="dt-row"><span class="dt-date">September 4, 2026</span><span class="dt-body"><a href="/events/jobs/2026-09-04-august-payrolls-rise-162000/">August report: payrolls up 162,000, unemployment rate 4.1 percent</a></span></div><div class="dt-row"><span class="dt-date">October 1, 2026</span><span class="dt-body">Before the release, economists surveyed by Dow Jones expected 84,000 jobs and a 4.1 percent rate</span></div><div class="dt-row"><span class="dt-date">October 2, 2026</span><span class="dt-body">Before 8:30 a.m. Eastern, economists surveyed by Reuters expected 90,000 jobs and a 4.1 percent rate</span></div><div class="dt-row dt-current"><span class="dt-date">October 2, 2026, 8:30 a.m. Eastern</span><span class="dt-body">The bureau publishes the September report: payrolls up 29,000, unemployment rate 4.2 percent</span></div><div class="dt-row"><span class="dt-date">November 6, 2026, 8:30 a.m. Eastern</span><span class="dt-body">Next Employment Situation, for October</span></div></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="bls-empsit-2026-09"><p>The Employment Situation for October is scheduled for Friday, November 6, 2026, at 8:30 a.m. Eastern. The bureau's release calendar lists the same morning.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="The Employment Situation for October 2026 is scheduled to be published on Friday, November 6, 2026, at 8:30 a.m. (ET)." aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-3" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-schedule-2026-10-03/" data-passage="October 2026 | Nov. 06, 2026 | 08:30 AM" aria-label="Source 3: read the evidence">3</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="ap-september-jobs"><p>The Associated Press wrote that the government reported the figures on Friday, &ldquo;a month before voters go to the polls in pivotal midterm elections&rdquo;.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-23" data-record-href="/records/ap-september-jobs/" data-passage="a month before voters go to the polls in pivotal midterm elections" aria-label="Source 23: read the evidence">23</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-checked">checked</span></div></section><section id="records"><h2 data-layer="fact">The records</h2><section id="records" data-layer="fact"><p class="sec-kicker">Sources</p><h2>The records</h2><p class="section-lede">Everything this page rests on, numbered where the story cites it. We keep a dated copy of each one.</p><h3 class="src-group">Primary documents</h3><ol class="src-list" start="1"><li id="src-1" value="1"><span class="src-n">1</span><a href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/">Employment Situation, September 2026</a> · released October 2, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the release&#39;s own payroll, unemployment, revision, industry, hours, and earnings figures</span></li><li id="src-2" value="2"><span class="src-n">2</span><a href="/records/bls-empsit-t09-2026-09/">Table A-9, multiple jobholders</a> · released October 2, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the count of people working more than one job</span></li><li id="src-3" value="3"><span class="src-n">3</span><a href="/records/bls-empsit-schedule-2026-10-03/">Employment Situation release calendar</a> · read October 3, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the publication time and the next report</span></li><li id="src-4" value="4"><span class="src-n">4</span><a href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-08/">Employment Situation, August 2026</a> · released September 4, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the August gain this release revises</span></li><li id="src-5" value="5"><span class="src-n">5</span><a href="/records/fomc-statement-2026-09-16/">FOMC statement, September 16, 2026</a> · released September 16, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the quarter-point increase that preceded this jobs report</span></li><li id="src-6" value="6"><span class="src-n">6</span><a href="/records/fomc-longer-run-goals-2025/">Longer-run goals and monetary policy strategy</a> · reaffirmed January 27, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the two goals the committee weighs, maximum employment and price stability</span></li><li id="src-7" value="7"><span class="src-n">7</span><a href="/records/bea-pce-2026-08/">Personal income and outlays, August 2026</a> · released September 30, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; August PCE inflation, 3.4 percent over the year</span></li><li id="src-8" value="8"><span class="src-n">8</span><a href="/records/bls-jolts-2026-08/">Job openings and labor turnover, August 2026</a> · released September 29, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; August hires and the date of the September hiring release</span></li><li id="src-9" value="9"><span class="src-n">9</span><a href="/records/fomc-calendar-2026-09-16/">FOMC calendar</a> · last updated September 16, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the October 27-28 meeting on the 2026 list</span></li></ol><h3 class="src-group">Official data</h3><ol class="src-list" start="10"><li id="src-10" value="10"><span class="src-n">10</span><a href="/records/fred-payems-2026-10-03/">Nonfarm payrolls, monthly series</a> · saved October 3, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the levels behind the chart and the computed averages</span></li><li id="src-11" value="11"><span class="src-n">11</span><a href="/records/fred-unrate-2026-10-03/">Unemployment rate, monthly series</a> · saved October 3, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the rate series through September</span></li><li id="src-12" value="12"><span class="src-n">12</span><a href="/records/fred-u6-2026-10-03/">U-6 rate, monthly series</a> · saved October 3, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the broad underemployment rate</span></li><li id="src-13" value="13"><span class="src-n">13</span><a href="/records/fred-prime-age-2026-10-03/">Prime-age participation, monthly series</a> · saved October 3, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; participation for ages 25 to 54</span></li><li id="src-14" value="14"><span class="src-n">14</span><a href="/records/fred-ahe-2026-10-03/">Average hourly earnings, monthly series</a> · saved October 3, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the wage level and the computed year-over-year change</span></li><li id="src-15" value="15"><span class="src-n">15</span><a href="/records/alfred-payems-2026-10-02/">Payrolls as of the release day</a> · vintage October 2, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the release-day payroll level, which matches the series saved the next morning</span></li><li id="src-16" value="16"><span class="src-n">16</span><a href="/records/adp-september-2026/">ADP National Employment Report, September 2026</a> · released September 30, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; a separate private-employment count of 90,000</span></li><li id="src-17" value="17"><span class="src-n">17</span><a href="/records/stlouisfed-unrate-2026-10/">St. Louis Fed flash report, October 2, 2026</a> · published October 2, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the unemployment rate before rounding</span></li></ol><h3 class="src-group">Coverage checked</h3><ol class="src-list" start="18"><li id="src-18" value="18"><span class="src-n">18</span><a href="/records/cnbc-september-jobs/">CNBC</a> · October 2, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the Dow Jones survey of 84,000, and a workweek figure the release does not support</span></li><li id="src-19" value="19"><span class="src-n">19</span><a href="/records/cnbc-september-jobs-preview/">CNBC preview</a> · October 1, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the same Dow Jones survey, published the day before</span></li><li id="src-20" value="20"><span class="src-n">20</span><a href="/records/reuters-september-jobs-preview/">Reuters</a> · October 2, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; a survey of 90,000 jobs, published before the release</span></li><li id="src-21" value="21"><span class="src-n">21</span><a href="/records/abc-september-jobs-preview/">ABC News</a> · October 2, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; a preview expecting 84,000 jobs</span></li><li id="src-22" value="22"><span class="src-n">22</span><a href="/records/cbs-september-jobs/">CBS News</a> · October 2, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; a FactSet forecast of 90,000</span></li><li id="src-23" value="23"><span class="src-n">23</span><a href="/records/ap-september-jobs/">Associated Press</a> · October 2, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the post-release dispatch, including an expectation of about 90,000</span></li><li id="src-24" value="24"><span class="src-n">24</span><a href="/records/pbs-september-jobs/">PBS NewsHour</a> · October 2, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the same Associated Press dispatch, carried by PBS</span></li><li id="src-25" value="25"><span class="src-n">25</span><a href="/records/usatoday-september-jobs/">USA Today</a> · October 2, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; USA Today&#39;s account of the 29,000 gain</span></li><li id="src-26" value="26"><span class="src-n">26</span><a href="/records/npr-september-jobs/">NPR</a> · October 2, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; NPR&#39;s account of the 29,000 gain</span></li><li id="src-27" value="27"><span class="src-n">27</span><a href="/records/upi-september-jobs/">UPI</a> · October 2, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; UPI&#39;s account of the 4.2 percent rate</span></li><li id="src-28" value="28"><span class="src-n">28</span><a href="/records/thehill-september-jobs/">The Hill</a> · October 2, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; The Hill&#39;s account of the 4.2 percent rate</span></li></ol><p class="sources-line"><a href="/methodology">How we check</a><span data-layer="proof"> &middot; where this page says <em>computed</em>, the arithmetic was done from the saved data series; where it says <em>record</em> or <em>checked</em>, the words trace to a saved document</span>.</p></section></section><details class="record-ledger" data-record-ledger data-layer="fact" data-state-derived><summary>Figures in the source documents <span class="series-count">7</span></summary><article class="ledger-row" data-state-slot="slot-86e3ee09-2145-42ee-8e6a-d72d64976d99"><h3>July and August combined, revised lower</h3><strong class="ledger-value" data-state-figure="story-figure:8699e92dc06c94e226875dddeb3485b37b53370c03b7b93cb5ddd4b94ac01684" data-state-figure-role="current" data-figure-value="60000" data-figure-unit="jobs">60,000 jobs</strong><a class="ledger-source" href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="With these revisions, employment in July and August combined is 60,000 lower than previously reported." aria-label="Read the source for July and August combined, revised lower">Bureau of Labor Statistics <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><details class="ledger-source-passage"><summary>Recorded passage</summary><blockquote data-source-copy>With these revisions, employment in July and August combined is 60,000 lower than previously reported.</blockquote></details></article><article class="ledger-row" data-state-slot="slot-92447502-d32f-4e80-be99-450d4676ef0b"><h3>Dow Jones survey of job growth</h3><strong class="ledger-value" data-state-figure="story-figure:14f4c30a65823f2e79455cf2859066bb494699ddc535e8c381c10e23f2750b45" data-state-figure-role="current" data-figure-value="84000" data-figure-unit="jobs">84,000 jobs</strong><a class="ledger-source" href="/records/cnbc-september-jobs/" data-passage="Economists surveyed by Dow Jones had been looking for job growth of 84,000 and an unemployment rate of 4.1%." aria-label="Read the source for Dow Jones survey of job growth">CNBC <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><details class="ledger-source-passage"><summary>Recorded passage</summary><blockquote data-source-copy>Economists surveyed by Dow Jones had been looking for job growth of 84,000 and an unemployment rate of 4.1%.</blockquote></details></article><article class="ledger-row" data-state-slot="slot-174ff606-1d3f-4871-b904-c0035a154ec6"><h3>Reuters survey of job growth</h3><strong class="ledger-value" data-state-figure="story-figure:5a0fd3e98eaa23181435d42be4ee65d4b13eface99aa42443e9c247cbabf19ef" data-state-figure-role="current" data-figure-value="90000" data-figure-unit="jobs">90,000 jobs</strong><a class="ledger-source" href="/records/reuters-september-jobs-preview/" data-passage="Nonfarm payrolls likely increased by 90,000 last month after surging by 162,000 in August, a Reuters survey of economists predicted." aria-label="Read the source for Reuters survey of job growth">Reuters <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><details class="ledger-source-passage"><summary>Recorded passage</summary><blockquote data-source-copy>Nonfarm payrolls likely increased by 90,000 last month after surging by 162,000 in August, a Reuters survey of economists predicted.</blockquote></details></article><article class="ledger-row" data-state-slot="slot-7c8548ac-4044-4e1a-8fee-e6f3a7e4198f"><h3>Payrolls added in September</h3><strong class="ledger-value" data-state-figure="story-figure:cfe045674b00f77eaaa4c91c1909a9bec9f948f3aaed056ef7118c054b1731c1" data-state-figure-role="current" data-figure-value="29000" data-figure-unit="jobs">29,000 jobs</strong><a class="ledger-source" href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="Total nonfarm payroll employment changed little in September (+29,000), following an average monthly gain of 45,000 over the prior 12 months." aria-label="Read the source for Payrolls added in September">Bureau of Labor Statistics <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><details class="ledger-source-passage"><summary>Recorded passage</summary><blockquote data-source-copy>Total nonfarm payroll employment changed little in September (+29,000), following an average monthly gain of 45,000 over the prior 12 months.</blockquote></details></article><article class="ledger-row" data-state-slot="slot-a7bc5aec-e010-4b38-a145-19bb9929d19e"><h3>Unemployment rate</h3><strong class="ledger-value" data-state-figure="story-figure:644101dd3aa52d9a3f807d7c6ba70ef40896e3b92683d32959a8207e75aac7bc" data-state-figure-role="current" data-figure-value="4.2" data-figure-unit="percent">4.2 percent</strong><a class="ledger-source" href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="Both the unemployment rate, at 4.2 percent, and the number of unemployed people, at 7.1 million, changed little in September." aria-label="Read the source for Unemployment rate">Bureau of Labor Statistics <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><details class="ledger-source-passage"><summary>Recorded passage</summary><blockquote data-source-copy>Both the unemployment rate, at 4.2 percent, and the number of unemployed people, at 7.1 million, changed little in September.</blockquote></details></article><article class="ledger-row" data-state-slot="slot-d3bf867c-f576-4c7d-b9a4-aacbbf2fefc6"><h3>Hourly earnings over the past year</h3><strong class="ledger-value" data-state-figure="story-figure:2bfcc3b03a9ba86f7940cf740435774ada6d99927d55c5c633935ae84cf30a22" data-state-figure-role="current" data-figure-value="3.0" data-figure-unit="percent">3.0 percent</strong><a class="ledger-source" href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="Over the past 12 months, average hourly earnings have increased by 3.0 percent." aria-label="Read the source for Hourly earnings over the past year">Bureau of Labor Statistics <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><details class="ledger-source-passage"><summary>Recorded passage</summary><blockquote data-source-copy>Over the past 12 months, average hourly earnings have increased by 3.0 percent.</blockquote></details></article><article class="ledger-row" data-state-slot="slot-7818ed42-a846-4a96-b907-8cc57d42a248"><h3>Average monthly gain over the prior 12 months</h3><strong class="ledger-value" data-state-figure="story-figure:0878c10bf3c23ddd524ac53722c19640275062285227ed61e67f9f3d52ab5df8" data-state-figure-role="current" data-figure-value="45000" data-figure-unit="jobs">45,000 jobs</strong><a class="ledger-source" href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="Total nonfarm payroll employment changed little in September (+29,000), following an average monthly gain of 45,000 over the prior 12 months." aria-label="Read the source for Average monthly gain over the prior 12 months">Bureau of Labor Statistics <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><details class="ledger-source-passage"><summary>Recorded passage</summary><blockquote data-source-copy>Total nonfarm payroll employment changed little in September (+29,000), following an average monthly gain of 45,000 over the prior 12 months.</blockquote></details></article></details><section data-layer="fact"><h2>Since the previous story</h2><p><a href="/events/jobs/2026-09-04-august-payrolls-rise-162000/">Read the previous story</a></p><p>July and August combined, revised lower: first tracked in this story. <span data-source-copy>With these revisions, employment in July and August combined is 60,000 lower than previously reported.</span></p><p>Dow Jones survey of job growth: first tracked in this story. <span data-source-copy>Economists surveyed by Dow Jones had been looking for job growth of 84,000 and an unemployment rate of 4.1%.</span></p><p>Reuters survey of job growth: first tracked in this story. <span data-source-copy>Nonfarm payrolls likely increased by 90,000 last month after surging by 162,000 in August, a Reuters survey of economists predicted.</span></p><p>Payrolls added in September: first tracked in this story. <span data-source-copy>Total nonfarm payroll employment changed little in September (+29,000), following an average monthly gain of 45,000 over the prior 12 months.</span></p><p>Unemployment rate: first tracked in this story. <span data-source-copy>Both the unemployment rate, at 4.2 percent, and the number of unemployed people, at 7.1 million, changed little in September.</span></p><p>Hourly earnings over the past year: first tracked in this story. <span data-source-copy>Over the past 12 months, average hourly earnings have increased by 3.0 percent.</span></p><p>Average monthly gain over the prior 12 months: first tracked in this story. <span data-source-copy>Total nonfarm payroll employment changed little in September (+29,000), following an average monthly gain of 45,000 over the prior 12 months.</span></p></section><section class="revision-timeline" data-layer="fact" data-revision-confirmations="7" data-revision-changes="0"><details class="source-history"><summary>Source history</summary><ul><li data-revision-confirmation><span data-source-copy>“Both the unemployment rate, at 4.2 percent, and the number of unemployed people, at 7.1 million,”</span> <a href="https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/empsit_10022026.htm">Bureau of Labor Statistics</a> reported on October 2</li><li data-revision-confirmation><span data-source-copy>“Economists surveyed by Dow Jones had been looking for job growth of 84,000”</span> <a href="https://www.cnbc.com/2026/10/02/jobs-report-september-2026.html">CNBC</a> reported on October 2</li><li data-revision-confirmation><span data-source-copy>“Nonfarm payrolls likely increased by 90,000 last month”</span> <a href="https://www.reuters.com/business/us-job-growth-expected-slow-september-unemployment-rate-likely-steady-2026-10-02/">Reuters</a> reported on October 2</li><li data-revision-confirmation><span data-source-copy>“Over the past 12 months, average hourly earnings have increased by 3.0 percent.”</span> <a href="https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/empsit_10022026.htm">Bureau of Labor Statistics</a> reported on October 2</li><li data-revision-confirmation><span data-source-copy>“Total nonfarm payroll employment changed little in September (+29,000), following an average”</span> <a href="https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/empsit_10022026.htm">Bureau of Labor Statistics</a> reported on October 2</li><li data-revision-confirmation><span data-source-copy>“employment in July and August combined is 60,000 lower than previously reported”</span> <a href="https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/empsit_10022026.htm">Bureau of Labor Statistics</a> reported on October 2</li><li data-revision-confirmation><span data-source-copy>“monthly gain of 45,000 over the prior 12 months”</span> <a href="https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/empsit_10022026.htm">Bureau of Labor Statistics</a> reported on October 2</li></ul></details></section><section data-layer="proof"><h2>Sources</h2><ul><li><a href="/records/reuters-september-jobs-preview/" data-source-copy>Reuters</a> · October 2, 2026 · <span data-source-copy>U.S. job growth expected to slow in September</span></li><li><a href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-source-copy>Bureau of Labor Statistics</a> · October 2, 2026 · <span data-source-copy>The Employment Situation, September 2026</span></li><li><a href="/records/cnbc-september-jobs/" data-source-copy>CNBC</a> · October 2, 2026 · <span data-source-copy>Labor market faltered in September</span></li></ul></section></main></div><footer class="colophon"><p>Published records carry their review status on the page. Work that has not been published is clearly labeled. Corrections and withdrawals are permanent, dated, and public.</p><p><a href="/">The Catch</a> <a href="/methodology">How we check</a> <a href="/records/">Source library</a></p></footer></div><div id="evidence-preview" class="evidence-preview" popover="manual" role="tooltip"><p class="preview-title"></p><p class="preview-copy"></p><span>Click to read in context</span></div><dialog id="evidence-reader" class="evidence-reader" aria-labelledby="evidence-title"><header class="evidence-toolbar"><span>Source reader</span><div><button type="button" data-expand-evidence aria-label="Expand source reader" aria-pressed="false"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 9V4h5m6 0h5v5M4 15v5h5m6 0h5v-5"></path></svg></button><button type="button" data-close-evidence aria-label="Close source reader"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m6 6 12 12M18 6 6 18"></path></svg></button></div></header><div class="evidence-identity"><p class="evidence-status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p><h2 id="evidence-title">Source</h2><p class="evidence-byline"></p><p class="evidence-purpose"></p><div class="evidence-view-controls" hidden><button type="button" data-context-view aria-pressed="true">Passage in context</button><button type="button" data-full-view aria-pressed="false">Full saved text</button></div></div><div class="evidence-scroll">