<section id="what-happened">
<h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>
<p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="1">The Bureau of Labor Statistics reported on Friday, October 2, 2026, that payroll employment rose by 29,000 in September and the unemployment rate was 4.2 percent, and said both changed little.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="8:30 a.m. (ET) Friday, October 2, 2026" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="Both nonfarm payroll employment (+29,000) and the unemployment rate (4.2 percent) changed little in September">1</a>
</sup>
</p>
<p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="1">Private payrolls rose by 46,000 and government payrolls fell by 17,000.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="Total private | 68 | 28 | 89 | 46" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="Government | 8 | -38 | 44 | -17">1</a>
</sup>
</p>
<p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="1">The bureau also revised the two prior months down by 60,000 together: August from a gain of 162,000 to 133,000, and July from a gain of 21,000 to a loss of 10,000.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="The change in total nonfarm payroll employment for July was revised down by 31,000, from +21,000 to -10,000, and the change for August was revised down by 29,000, from +162,000 to +133,000." aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="employment in July and August combined is 60,000 lower than previously reported">1</a>
</sup>
</p>
<p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="4">The establishment survey counts jobs on employer payrolls. The household survey counts people, and individuals are counted only once. A jobs number and a people number can move differently in the same month. The payroll count comes from the first survey, and the unemployment rate from the second.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="The household survey measures labor force status, including unemployment, by demographic characteristics. The establishment survey measures nonfarm employment, hours, and earnings by industry." aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="individuals are counted only once">1</a>
</sup>
</p>
<p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="2">The Federal Reserve's two goals are maximum employment and price stability.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-6" data-record-href="/records/fomc-longer-run-goals-2025/" data-passage="maximum employment and price stability" aria-label="Source 6: read the evidence">6</a>
</sup> Its primary means of adjusting the stance of monetary policy is through changes in the target range for the federal funds rate.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-6" data-record-href="/records/fomc-longer-run-goals-2025/" data-passage="primary means of adjusting the stance of monetary policy is through changes in the target range for the federal funds rate" aria-label="Source 6: read the evidence">6</a>
</sup> On September 16 it raised that range by 1/4 percentage point<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-5" data-record-href="/records/fomc-statement-2026-09-16/" data-passage="The Committee decided to raise the target range for the federal funds rate by 1/4 percentage point to 3-3/4 to 4 percent" aria-label="Source 5: read the evidence">5</a>
</sup> in support of those goals.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-5" data-record-href="/records/fomc-statement-2026-09-16/" data-passage="in support of the Federal Reserve's dual mandate" aria-label="Source 5: read the evidence">5</a>
</sup> This report is the employment reading before that committee meets on October 27-28; the next one, for October, is scheduled for November 6.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-9" data-record-href="/records/fomc-calendar-2026-09-16/" data-passage="October 27-28" aria-label="Source 9: read the evidence">9</a>
</sup>
<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="The Employment Situation for October 2026 is scheduled to be published on Friday, November 6, 2026" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a>
</sup> On prices, the personal consumption expenditures price index for August, whose annual change is how the committee measures inflation,<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-6" data-record-href="/records/fomc-longer-run-goals-2025/" data-passage="as measured by the annual change in the price index for personal consumption expenditures" aria-label="Source 6: read the evidence">6</a>
</sup> was up 3.4 percent from a year earlier.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-7" data-record-href="/records/bea-pce-2026-08/" data-passage="From the same month one year ago, the PCE price index for August increased 3.4 percent." aria-label="Source 7: read the evidence">7</a>
</sup> This report put average hourly earnings up 3.0 percent over the past 12 months.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="Over the past 12 months, average hourly earnings have increased by 3.0 percent" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a>
</sup>
</p>
<div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="bls-empsit-2026-09">
<p>The Bureau of Labor Statistics reported on Friday, October 2, 2026, that payroll employment rose by 29,000 in September and the unemployment rate was 4.2 percent, and said both changed little. The release was embargoed until 8:30 a.m. Eastern that morning; the calendar schedules the same time. The unemployment rate has stayed between 4.1 percent and 4.3 percent since March, and 7.1 million people were unemployed.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="8:30 a.m. (ET) Friday, October 2, 2026" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="Both the unemployment rate, at 4.2 percent, and the number of unemployed people, at 7.1 million, changed little in September">1</a>
</sup>
<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-3" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-schedule-2026-10-03/" data-passage="September 2026 | Oct. 02, 2026 | 08:30 AM" aria-label="Source 3: read the evidence">3</a>
</sup>
</p>
<span class="chip chip-record">record</span>
</div>
<div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="bls-empsit-2026-09">
<p>July was revised down by 31,000, from a gain of 21,000 to a loss of 10,000. August was revised down by 29,000, from a gain of 162,000 to a gain of 133,000. The August release had reported a gain of 162,000. The two months together are 60,000 lower than previously reported.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="employment in July and August combined is 60,000 lower than previously reported" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a>
</sup>
<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-4" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-08/" data-passage="Total nonfarm payroll employment increased by 162,000 in August, and the unemployment rate was unchanged at 4.1 percent" aria-label="Source 4: read the evidence">4</a>
</sup>
</p>
<span class="chip chip-record">record</span>
</div>
<details class="receipt" data-layer="proof">
<summary>July and August, as the September release states them</summary>
<figure class="data-table-wrap">
<table class="data-table">
<caption>Changes in thousands of jobs, from the September release.</caption>
<thead>
<tr>
<th class>Month</th>
<th class>Previously reported</th>
<th class>September release</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td class>July</td>
<td class>+21,000</td>
<td class>-10,000</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td class>August</td>
<td class>+162,000</td>
<td class>+133,000</td>
</tr>
