</table>
</figure>
</details>
<div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="bls-empsit-2026-09">
<p>Employment in every major industry changed little. Health care added 17,000, slower than its average monthly gain of 33,000 over the prior 12 months. Ambulatory care added 13,000 and hospitals 12,000. Nursing and residential care lost 9,000. Construction added 11,000. Manufacturing added 9,000 and is up by 72,000 since a recent low in December 2025. Financial activities lost 7,000 and are down 129,000 since a peak in May 2025, most of that in insurance carriers and related activities.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="Health care employment continued its upward trend in September (+17,000)" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="Employment in all major industries changed little over the month&#10;nursing and residential care facilities lost jobs (-9,000)&#10;Manufacturing employment was little changed in September (+9,000) but is up by 72,000 since a recent low in December 2025.&#10;Employment in financial activities is down by 129,000 since a recent peak in May 2025&#10;insurance carriers and related activities (-90,000).">1</a>
</sup>
</p>
<span class="chip chip-record">record</span>
</div>
<div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="bls-empsit-2026-09">
<p>The one-month diffusion index for 250 private industries was 49.0 in September after 57.6 in August. The release counts it as the percent of industries with employment increasing plus one-half of the industries with unchanged employment, and says 50 percent is an equal balance between industries adding and cutting jobs.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="Figures are the percent of industries with employment increasing plus one-half of the industries with unchanged employment" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a>
</sup> Private payrolls rose by 46,000 and government payrolls fell by 17,000. Information fell by 10,000. Temporary help services fell by 10.9 thousand.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="Total private (250 industries) | 50.4 | 54.4 | 57.6 | 49.0" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="50 percent indicates an equal balance between industries with increasing and decreasing employment&#10;Total private | 68 | 28 | 89 | 46&#10;Government | 8 | -38 | 44 | -17&#10;Information | -1 | 4 | -18 | -10&#10;Temporary help services | -11.7 | -10.0 | 0.2 | -10.9">1</a>
</sup>
</p>
<span class="chip chip-record">record</span>
</div>
<div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="bls-empsit-2026-09">
<p>Average hourly earnings edged up 5 cents, or 0.1 percent, to $37.81. Over the year they are up 3.0 percent. The average workweek stayed at 34.4 hours. A change of about 122,000 jobs is what the bureau calls statistically significant in the employer survey, so the 29,000 gain does not clear that bar. The household survey's bar is about 650,000.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="average hourly earnings for all employees on private nonfarm payrolls edged up by 5 cents, or 0.1 percent, to $37.81" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="An over-the-month employment change of about 122,000 is statistically significant in the establishment survey">1</a>
</sup>
</p>
<span class="chip chip-record">record</span>
</div>
<div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="ap-september-jobs">
<p>The Associated Press called that yearly wage gain &ldquo;the smallest year-over-year gain since May 2021&rdquo;.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-23" data-record-href="/records/ap-september-jobs/" data-passage="the smallest year-over-year gain since May 2021" aria-label="Source 23: read the evidence">23</a>
</sup>
</p>
<span class="chip chip-checked">checked</span>
</div>
<div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="cnbc-september-jobs">
<p>CNBC wrote that the average workweek was unchanged at 34.6 hours. CNBC's 34.6 hours is not the release's figure. The release says the workweek remained at 34.4 hours.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-18" data-record-href="/records/cnbc-september-jobs/" data-passage="The average work week was unchanged at 34.6 hours" aria-label="Source 18: read the evidence">18</a>
</sup>
<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="remained at 34.4 hours in September" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a>
</sup>
</p>
<span class="chip chip-checked">checked</span>
</div>
<div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="bls-empsit-2026-09">
<p>The unemployment rate for Black workers rose to 7.0 percent. The bureau said that rate increased, and that the rates for adult men (3.9 percent), adult women (3.6 percent), teenagers (14.5 percent), and White (3.6), Asian (2.9), and Hispanic (4.7) workers showed little change. The labor force participation rate was 61.8 percent and the employment-population ratio was 59.2 percent. The labor force rose by 485,000 and household employment by 406,000.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="the unemployment rate for people who are Black (7.0 percent) increased in September" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="Both the labor force participation rate, at 61.8 percent, and the employment-population ratio, at 59.2 percent, changed little in September">1</a>
</sup>
</p>
<span class="chip chip-record">record</span>
</div>
<div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="bls-empsit-2026-09">
<p>Job losers and people who completed temporary jobs fell by 45,000. Job leavers fell by 173,000. Reentrants rose by 152,000 and new entrants rose by 116,000. The summary table counts those groups in thousands.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="Job losers and people who completed temporary jobs | 3,524 | 3,309 | 3,245 | 3,200 | -45" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="Job leavers | 860 | 793 | 914 | 741 | -173&#10;Reentrants | 2,336 | 2,123 | 2,137 | 2,289 | 152&#10;New entrants | 813 | 761 | 702 | 818 | 116">1</a>
</sup>
</p>
<span class="chip chip-record">record</span>
</div>
<div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="bls-empsit-2026-09">
<p>The long-term unemployed, out of work 27 weeks or more, were essentially unchanged at 1.9 million and were 27.1 percent of unemployed people. People employed part time for economic reasons were little changed at 4.5 million. They would have preferred full-time employment but were working part time because their hours had been reduced or they were unable to find full-time jobs.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="These individuals would have preferred full-time employment but were working part time because their hours had been reduced or they were unable to find full-time jobs." aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a>
</sup> People marginally attached to the labor force fell by 236,000 to 1.5 million. Discouraged workers were little changed at 414,000.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="The number of long-term unemployed (those jobless for 27 weeks or more) was essentially unchanged at 1.9 million in September" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="The number of people employed part time for economic reasons changed little at 4.5 million in September">1</a>
</sup>
</p>
<span class="chip chip-record">record</span>
</div>
<div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="bls-empsit-2026-09">
<p>The broader underemployment rate, U-6, was 7.6 percent in September, after 7.7 percent in August.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="U-6 Total unemployed, plus all people marginally attached to the labor force, plus total employed part time for economic reasons, as a percent of the civilian labor force plus all people marginally attached to the labor force | 7.7 | 7.8 | 7.3 | 8.1 | 8.1 | 7.9 | 7.9 | 7.7 | 7.6" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a>
</sup>
<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-12" data-record-href="/records/fred-u6-2026-10-03/" data-passage="2026-09-01,7.6" aria-label="Source 12: read the evidence">12</a>
</sup>
</p>
<span class="chip chip-record">record</span>
</div>
<div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="bls-empsit-t09-2026-09">
<p>The household survey counted 8,986,000 people working more than one job in September, 5.5 percent of everyone employed, against 8,805,000 in August. The table counts those people in thousands.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-2" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-t09-2026-09/" data-passage="Total multiple jobholders | 8,794 | 8,505 | 9,000 | 8,810 | 8,428 | 8,554 | 8,693 | 8,805 | 8,986" aria-label="Source 2: read the evidence">2</a>
</sup>
</p>
<span class="chip chip-record">record</span>
</div>
<div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="fred-prime-age-2026-10-03">
<p>Prime-age participation was 83.7 percent in September, up from 83.4 percent in August.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-13" data-record-href="/records/fred-prime-age-2026-10-03/" data-passage="2026-08-01,83.4" aria-label="Source 13: read the evidence" data-passages="2026-09-01,83.7">13</a>
</sup>
</p>
<span class="chip chip-official">official data</span>
</div>
<div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="fred-ahe-2026-10-03">
<p>On the hourly-earnings series, September's $37.81 is 3.02 percent above September 2025. The release rounds that change to 3.0 percent.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-14" data-record-href="/records/fred-ahe-2026-10-03/" data-passage="2026-09-01,37.81" aria-label="Source 14: read the evidence">14</a>
</sup>
<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="Over the past 12 months, average hourly earnings have increased by 3.0 percent" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a>
</sup>
</p>
<span class="chip chip-official">official data</span>
</div>
</section>
<section id="the-catch">
<h2 data-layer="fact">The catch</h2>
<p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="3">USA Today headlined the release &ldquo;September jobs report shows slowdown in hiring, unemployment ticks up&rdquo;.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-25" data-record-href="/records/usatoday-september-jobs/" data-passage="September jobs report shows slowdown in hiring, unemployment ticks up" aria-label="Source 25: read the evidence">25</a>
</sup> The bureau's table shows the rounded unemployment rate up from 4.1 to 4.2, and its summary says payrolls and that rate both &ldquo;changed little&rdquo;, which describes the size of the move.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="Unemployment rate | 4.4 | 4.1 | 4.1 | 4.2 | 0.1" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="Both nonfarm payroll employment (+29,000) and the unemployment rate (4.2 percent) changed little in September">1</a>
</sup> The 29,000 is a net change in payroll jobs, not a count of hires, and it is under about 122,000, the monthly change the bureau says is statistically significant.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="An over-the-month employment change of about 122,000 is statistically significant in the establishment survey" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="Total nonfarm payroll employment changed little in September (+29,000)">1</a>
</sup> Hires, &ldquo;all additions to the payroll&rdquo; during the month, are counted in a separate survey, and September's count is scheduled for November 3.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-8" data-record-href="/records/bls-jolts-2026-08/" data-passage="all additions to the payroll" aria-label="Source 8: read the evidence" data-passages="The Job Openings and Labor Turnover news release for September 2026 is scheduled to be published on Tuesday, November 3, 2026, at 10:00 a.m. (ET).">8</a>
</sup>
</p>
<p data-layer="narrative">By the household survey's estimates, employment rose by 406,000, which is under the 650,000 that survey needs before a change is statistically significant.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="Employed | 163,656 | 162,177 | 162,746 | 163,152 | 406" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="An over-the-month employment change of about 122,000 is statistically significant in the establishment survey, while the threshold for a statistically significant change in the household survey is about 650,000.">1</a>
</sup> The labor force, the employed plus the unemployed, rose by 485,000.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="Civilian labor force | 171,261 | 169,094 | 169,777 | 170,262 | 485" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="The civilian labor force is the sum of the employed and unemployed">1</a>
</sup> The unemployment rate is the number unemployed as a percent of the labor force. The labor force grew by more than employment did, so the count of unemployed rose by 78,000 and the rate went from 4.1 to 4.2 percent.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="The unemployment rate is the number unemployed as a percent of the labor force" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="Unemployed | 7,605 | 6,916 | 7,031 | 7,109 | 78&#10;Unemployment rate | 4.4 | 4.1 | 4.1 | 4.2 | 0.1">1</a>
</sup>
</p>
<p data-layer="narrative">Most of that tenth is rounding. Before rounding, the rate &ldquo;increased slightly from 4.141% to 4.175%&rdquo;, the St. Louis Fed wrote on October 2, a rise of about 0.03 point.<sup class="src-ref">

exec
/bin/zsh -lc "sed 's/></>\\n</g' dist/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000/index.html | sed -n '341,680p'" in /Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept
 succeeded in 0ms:
<a href="#src-17" data-record-href="/records/stlouisfed-unrate-2026-10/" data-passage="More precise data show the rate increased slightly from 4.141% to 4.175%." aria-label="Source 17: read the evidence" data-passages="St. Louis Fed On the Economy, Oct. 2, 2026.">17</a>
</sup> The rate has stayed between 4.1 percent and 4.3 percent since March.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="The unemployment rate has remained in a narrow range of 4.1 percent to 4.3 percent since March." aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a>
</sup>
</p>
<p data-layer="narrative">The bureau named one group whose rate increased: Black workers, from 6.0 percent in August to 7.0 percent in September.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="the unemployment rate for people who are Black (7.0 percent) increased" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="Black or African American | 7.6 | 6.3 | 6.0 | 7.0 | 1.0&#10;Unemployment rate | 4.4 | 4.1 | 4.1 | 4.2 | 0.1">1</a>
</sup> A year earlier it was 7.6 percent.</p>
</section>
