<p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="5">September's 29,000 followed August's revised 133,000.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="the change for August was revised down by 29,000, from +162,000 to +133,000" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a>
</sup> The bureau's 45,000 a month is its average over the prior 12 months, and September is lower.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="following an average monthly gain of 45,000 over the prior 12 months" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a>
</sup> The Associated Press's 68,000 a month is 2026 so far,<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-23" data-record-href="/records/ap-september-jobs/" data-passage="average 68,000 jobs a month so far this year" aria-label="Source 23: read the evidence">23</a>
</sup> and its 9,700 is 2025.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-23" data-record-href="/records/ap-september-jobs/" data-passage="9,700 average new jobs created every month in 2025" aria-label="Source 23: read the evidence">23</a>
</sup> The three averages cover different months, which is why they are not the same pace.</p>
<div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="bls-empsit-2026-09">
<p>September's 29,000 followed an average monthly gain of 45,000 over the prior 12 months, the figure the release states. Its three-month average line puts July through September at 51,000.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="following an average monthly gain of 45,000 over the prior 12 months" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="Total nonfarm | 23 | 28 | 51 | 51">1</a>
</sup>
</p>
<span class="chip chip-record">record</span>
</div>
<div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="fred-payems-2026-10-03">
<p>On the current payroll series, the twelve monthly changes through August average 45,250 jobs. 4 of the last twelve months were declines. The nine months of 2026 average 68,000 jobs. The series prints 159044 for September and 159015 for August.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-10" data-record-href="/records/fred-payems-2026-10-03/" data-passage="2026-09-01,159044" aria-label="Source 10: read the evidence" data-passages="2026-08-01,159015">10</a>
</sup> Table B-1 counts employees on nonfarm payrolls in thousands and prints 159,015 and 159,044. The difference of 29 in that table is the release's change of 29,000.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="Table B-1. Employees on nonfarm payrolls by industry sector and selected industry detail" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="[In thousands]&#10;Total nonfarm | 158,729 | 158,709 | 158,905 | 159,238 | 158,548 | 158,882 | 159,015 | 159,044 | 29&#10;Both nonfarm payroll employment (+29,000) and the unemployment rate (4.2 percent) changed little in September">1</a>
</sup>
</p>
<span class="chip chip-computed">computed</span>
</div>
<details class="receipt" data-layer="proof">
<summary>How those averages were counted</summary>
<p>Each month is that month's payroll total minus the month before, from the series saved October 3. The twelve months before September, September 2025 through August 2026, sum to 543 thousand and divide by 12 to 45,250. The release states that average as 45,000. The last twelve months include four declines: October 2025, December 2025, February 2026, and July 2026. The 2026 average is January through September, 612 thousand divided by 9.</p>
</details>
<div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="cnbc-september-jobs">
<p>CNBC reported that economists surveyed by Dow Jones had looked for job growth of 84,000 and an unemployment rate of 4.1 percent. Reuters, writing before the release, said its survey of economists expected 90,000 jobs and a 4.1 percent rate for a third month. Estimates ranged from as low as 35,000 to as high as 180,000. CBS reported a FactSet forecast of 90,000. The Associated Press said economists had expected about 90,000.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-18" data-record-href="/records/cnbc-september-jobs/" data-passage="Economists surveyed by Dow Jones had been looking for job growth of 84,000 and an unemployment rate of 4.1%" aria-label="Source 18: read the evidence">18</a>
</sup>
<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-20" data-record-href="/records/reuters-september-jobs-preview/" data-passage="Nonfarm payrolls likely increased by 90,000 last month" aria-label="Source 20: read the evidence" data-passages="Estimates ranged from as low as 35,000 to as high as 180,000.">20</a>
</sup>
<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-22" data-record-href="/records/cbs-september-jobs/" data-passage="Economists had forecast the economy would add 90,000 new jobs last month, according to financial data firm FactSet" aria-label="Source 22: read the evidence">22</a>
</sup>
<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-23" data-record-href="/records/ap-september-jobs/" data-passage="Economists had expected September payrolls to come in around 90,000" aria-label="Source 23: read the evidence">23</a>
</sup>
</p>
<span class="chip chip-record">record</span>
</div>
</section>
<section id="unknowns">
<h2 data-layer="fact">What we do not know yet</h2>
<p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="6">The payroll figures for August and September are preliminary: the bureau says they rest on incomplete returns from the employers it surveys.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="estimates for the most recent 2 months are based on incomplete returns; for this reason, these estimates are labeled preliminary" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a>
</sup> The release states no cause for the 29,000 change. Whether hiring slowed is not known yet: hires are counted separately, August's hires were 5.2 million, and September's are scheduled for November 3.<sup class="src-ref">
<a href="#src-8" data-record-href="/records/bls-jolts-2026-08/" data-passage="Hires changed little at 5.2 million" aria-label="Source 8: read the evidence" data-passages="The Job Openings and Labor Turnover news release for September 2026 is scheduled to be published on Tuesday, November 3, 2026, at 10:00 a.m. (ET).">8</a>
</sup>
</p>
</section>
