src/pages/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.astro:26:      <p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">{event.dek}<Cite s="reuters-september-jobs-preview" passage="Nonfarm payrolls likely increased by 90,000 last month" /><Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="Both nonfarm payroll employment (+29,000) and the unemployment rate (4.2 percent) changed little in September" /><Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="employment in July and August combined is 60,000 lower than previously reported" /></p>
src/pages/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.astro:52:        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="1">The Bureau of Labor Statistics reported on Friday, October 2, 2026, that payroll employment rose by {jobs(septemberPayrollChange * 1000)} in September and the unemployment rate was {household.unemploymentRate} percent, and said both changed little.<Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="8:30 a.m. (ET) Friday, October 2, 2026" /><Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="Both nonfarm payroll employment (+29,000) and the unemployment rate (4.2 percent) changed little in September" /></p>
src/pages/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.astro:54:        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="1">The bureau also revised the two prior months down by {jobs(Math.abs(revisions.combined) * 1000)} together: August from a gain of {jobs(revisions.augustFrom * 1000)} to {jobs(revisions.augustTo * 1000)}, and July from a gain of {jobs(revisions.julyFrom * 1000)} to a loss of {jobs(Math.abs(revisions.julyTo) * 1000)}.<Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="The change in total nonfarm payroll employment for July was revised down by 31,000, from +21,000 to -10,000, and the change for August was revised down by 29,000, from +162,000 to +133,000." /><Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="employment in July and August combined is 60,000 lower than previously reported" /></p>
src/pages/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.astro:58:          <p>The Bureau of Labor Statistics reported on Friday, October 2, 2026, that payroll employment rose by 29,000 in September and the unemployment rate was 4.2 percent, and said both changed little. The release was embargoed until 8:30 a.m. Eastern that morning; the calendar schedules the same time. The unemployment rate has stayed between 4.1 percent and 4.3 percent since March, and 7.1 million people were unemployed.<Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="8:30 a.m. (ET) Friday, October 2, 2026" /><Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="Both the unemployment rate, at 4.2 percent, and the number of unemployed people, at 7.1 million, changed little in September" /><Cite s="bls-empsit-schedule-2026-10-03" passage="September 2026 | Oct. 02, 2026 | 08:30 AM" /></p>
src/pages/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.astro:61:          <p>July was revised down by 31,000, from a gain of 21,000 to a loss of 10,000. August was revised down by 29,000, from a gain of 162,000 to a gain of 133,000. The August release had reported a gain of 162,000. The two months together are 60,000 lower than previously reported.<Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="employment in July and August combined is 60,000 lower than previously reported" /><Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-08" passage="Total nonfarm payroll employment increased by 162,000 in August, and the unemployment rate was unchanged at 4.1 percent" /></p>
src/pages/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.astro:68:          <p>Employment in every major industry changed little. Health care added 17,000, slower than its average monthly gain of 33,000 over the prior 12 months. Ambulatory care added 13,000 and hospitals 12,000. Nursing and residential care lost 9,000. Construction added 11,000. Manufacturing added 9,000 and is up by 72,000 since a recent low in December 2025. Financial activities lost 7,000 and are down 129,000 since a peak in May 2025, most of that in insurance carriers and related activities.<Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="Health care employment continued its upward trend in September (+17,000)" /><Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="Employment in all major industries changed little over the month" /><Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="nursing and residential care facilities lost jobs (-9,000)" /><Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="Manufacturing employment was little changed in September (+9,000) but is up by 72,000 since a recent low in December 2025." /><Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="Employment in financial activities is down by 129,000 since a recent peak in May 2025" /><Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="insurance carriers and related activities (-90,000)." /></p>
src/pages/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.astro:74:          <p>Average hourly earnings edged up 5 cents, or 0.1 percent, to $37.81. Over the year they are up 3.0 percent. The average workweek stayed at 34.4 hours. A change of about 122,000 jobs is what the bureau calls statistically significant in the employer survey, so the 29,000 gain does not clear that bar. The household survey's bar is about 650,000.<Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="average hourly earnings for all employees on private nonfarm payrolls edged up by 5 cents, or 0.1 percent, to $37.81" /><Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="An over-the-month employment change of about 122,000 is statistically significant in the establishment survey" /></p>
src/pages/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.astro:107:        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="3">USA Today headlined the release &ldquo;September jobs report shows slowdown in hiring, unemployment ticks up&rdquo;.<Cite s="usatoday-september-jobs" passage="September jobs report shows slowdown in hiring, unemployment ticks up" /> The bureau's table shows the rounded unemployment rate up from 4.1 to 4.2, and its summary says payrolls and that rate both &ldquo;changed little&rdquo;, which describes the size of the move.<Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="Unemployment rate | 4.4 | 4.1 | 4.1 | 4.2 | 0.1" /><Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="Both nonfarm payroll employment (+29,000) and the unemployment rate (4.2 percent) changed little in September" /> The {jobs(septemberPayrollChange * 1000)} is a net change in payroll jobs, not a count of hires, and it is under about {jobs(establishment.significanceThreshold * 1000)}, the monthly change the bureau says is statistically significant.<Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="An over-the-month employment change of about 122,000 is statistically significant in the establishment survey" /><Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="Total nonfarm payroll employment changed little in September (+29,000)" /> Hires, &ldquo;all additions to the payroll&rdquo; during the month, are counted in a separate survey, and September's count is scheduled for November 3.<Cite s="bls-jolts-2026-08" passage="all additions to the payroll" /><Cite s="bls-jolts-2026-08" passage="The Job Openings and Labor Turnover news release for September 2026 is scheduled to be published on Tuesday, November 3, 2026, at 10:00 a.m. (ET)." /></p>
src/pages/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.astro:116:        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="5">September's {jobs(septemberPayrollChange * 1000)} followed August's revised {jobs(revisions.augustTo * 1000)}.<Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="the change for August was revised down by 29,000, from +162,000 to +133,000" /> The bureau's {jobs(averages.prior12AsReleased * 1000)} a month is its average over the prior 12 months, and September is lower.<Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="following an average monthly gain of 45,000 over the prior 12 months" /> The Associated Press's {jobs(averages.year2026 * 1000)} a month is 2026 so far,<Cite s="ap-september-jobs" passage="average 68,000 jobs a month so far this year" /> and its {jobs(admitted.apYear2025)} is 2025.<Cite s="ap-september-jobs" passage="9,700 average new jobs created every month in 2025" /> The three averages cover different months, which is why they are not the same pace.</p>
src/pages/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.astro:118:          <p>September's 29,000 followed an average monthly gain of 45,000 over the prior 12 months, the figure the release states. Its three-month average line puts July through September at 51,000.<Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="following an average monthly gain of 45,000 over the prior 12 months" /><Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="Total nonfarm | 23 | 28 | 51 | 51" /></p>
src/pages/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.astro:121:          <p>On the current payroll series, the twelve monthly changes through August average {prior12Jobs} jobs. {averages.negativeMonthsLast12} of the last twelve months were declines. The nine months of 2026 average {yearJobs} jobs. The series prints 159044 for September and 159015 for August.<Cite s="fred-payems-2026-10-03" passage="2026-09-01,159044" /><Cite s="fred-payems-2026-10-03" passage="2026-08-01,159015" /> Table B-1 counts employees on nonfarm payrolls in thousands and prints 159,015 and 159,044. The difference of 29 in that table is the release's change of 29,000.<Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="Table B-1. Employees on nonfarm payrolls by industry sector and selected industry detail" /><Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="[In thousands]" /><Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="Total nonfarm | 158,729 | 158,709 | 158,905 | 159,238 | 158,548 | 158,882 | 159,015 | 159,044 | 29" /><Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="Both nonfarm payroll employment (+29,000) and the unemployment rate (4.2 percent) changed little in September" /></p>
