32:| `bls-empsit-2026-09` | "Both nonfarm payroll employment (+29,000) and the unemployment rate (4.2 percent) changed little in September" Audit finding 1's number. The act and the bureau's own "changed little." | A | 1, the estimate is 29,000 and a 4.2 percent rate the bureau says changed little |
34:| `bls-empsit-2026-09` | "The unemployment rate has remained in a narrow range of 4.1 percent to 4.3 percent since March." The 7.1 million unemployed is the same release's "changed little" count. | B | 5, the 4.2 percent rate is inside the 4.1 to 4.3 range held since March |
35:| `bls-empsit-2026-09` | "the unemployment rate for people who are Black (7.0 percent) increased" "Black or African American \| 7.6 \| 6.3 \| 6.0 \| 7.0 \| 1.0" Audit finding 3. | B | 3, Black unemployment rose by a point, to 7.0 percent, and was 7.6 a year earlier |
41:| `bls-empsit-2026-09` | "following an average monthly gain of 45,000 over the prior 12 months" The +29,000 in this same sentence is the lead line. | B | 5, September's 29,000 is below the release's prior-12-month average of 45,000 |
47:| `bls-empsit-2026-09` | "Over the past 12 months, average hourly earnings have increased by 3.0 percent" The 5 cent, 0.1 percent move is the same passage and is not a second fact. | B | 2, pay over the past year is up 3.0 percent |
49:| `bls-empsit-2026-09` | "employment in July and August combined is 60,000 lower than previously reported" July from +21,000 to -10,000 and August from +162,000 to +133,000 are in the same sentence. | A | 1, July and August together are 60,000 lower than previously reported |
50:| `bls-empsit-2026-09` | "The Employment Situation for October 2026 is scheduled to be published on Friday, November 6, 2026, at 8:30 a.m. (ET)." A scheduled next step, not an unknown. | B | 6, the next employment report is scheduled for November 6 and was not out |
51:| `bls-empsit-2026-09` | "An over-the-month employment change of about 122,000 is statistically significant in the establishment survey, while the threshold for a statistically significant change in the household survey is about 650,000." Audit finding 1. | B | 3, a gain of 29,000 does not clear the bar the bureau calls statistically significant |
52:| `bls-empsit-2026-09` | "Total private \| 68 \| 28 \| 89 \| 46" "Government \| 8 \| -38 \| 44 \| -17" Audit finding 5's establishment split. Diffusion 49.0 and the three-month average of 51,000 are the same passage-table line and are not this clause. | A | 1, the 29,000 is private payrolls up 46,000 and government down 17,000 |
57:| `bls-empsit-schedule-2026-10-03` | "September 2026 \| Oct. 02, 2026 \| 08:30 AM" Audit finding 1's date is this morning. | A | 1, the estimate was published at 8:30 a.m. Eastern on October 2 |
59:| `bls-empsit-2026-08` | "Total nonfarm payroll employment increased by 162,000 in August, and the unemployment rate was unchanged at 4.1 percent" The September release restates the old August figure inside the revision sentence. This line is the prior release's own words. | A | 5, the month before was first reported as a 162,000 gain and a 4.1 percent rate |
78:| `bls-empsit-2026-09` | "The household survey measures labor force status, including unemployment, by demographic characteristics. The establishment survey measures nonfarm employment, hours, and earnings by industry." The mechanism. | B | 4, the payroll count and the unemployment rate come from two different surveys |
79:| `bls-empsit-2026-09` | "estimates for the most recent 2 months are based on incomplete returns; for this reason, these estimates are labeled preliminary" Audit finding 1, the technical note. The newest two months are not final. | B | 6, September's 29,000 is still labeled preliminary |
80:| `bls-empsit-2026-09` | "Employed \| 163,656 \| 162,177 \| 162,746 \| 163,152 \| 406" Audit finding 2. Gap line L336 is this September level. The change column is 406 thousand. | B | 3, household employment rose by 406,000 in the month the rate ticked up |
81:| `bls-empsit-2026-09` | "Civilian labor force \| 171,261 \| 169,094 \| 169,777 \| 170,262 \| 485" Audit finding 2. Gap line L334 is this September level. The change column is 485 thousand. | B | 3, the labor force grew by 485,000, so the rate did not rise because people left work |
647:| `bls-jolts-2026-08` | "Hires changed little at 5.2 million" "The Job Openings and Labor Turnover news release for September 2026 is scheduled to be published on Tuesday, November 3, 2026, at 10:00 a.m. (ET)." Admitted in the story turn. The audit named the PDF; the pin is the release-day HTML of the same release. | B | 6, September gross hires had not been published, and the latest hires figure changed little |
648:| `stlouisfed-unrate-2026-10` | "More precise data show the rate increased slightly from 4.141% to 4.175%." Admitted in the story turn. The note's own figures are 4.141 percent and 4.175 percent, not a longer unrounded string. | B | 3, the rounded move from 4.1 to 4.2 is a few hundredths of a point |
649:| `fomc-statement-2026-09-16` | "The Committee decided to raise the target range for the federal funds rate by 1/4 percentage point to 3-3/4 to 4 percent" Admitted in the story turn. The pin was already saved with the September rate story. | B | 2, the jobs estimate arrives after the committee's own quarter-point increase on September 16 |
650:| `fomc-longer-run-goals-2025` | "maximum employment and price stability" The September 16 statement says the increase was in support of the dual mandate and does not name the two goals. This statement does. | B | 2, the committee weighs employment against prices |
651:| `fomc-calendar-2026-09-16` | "October 27-28" Admitted in the story turn. On the 2026 list that date follows September 15-16, and the page shows a statement for September and none for October. | B | 6, the next policy meeting is October 27-28 and no decision is in the record |
652:| `bea-pce-2026-08` | "From the same month one year ago, the PCE price index for August increased 3.4 percent." Admitted in the story turn. The release line is Wednesday, September 30, 2026. | B | 2, August PCE inflation, the other number in front of the next decision, was 3.4 percent |
663:| `usatoday-september-jobs` | "September jobs report shows slowdown in hiring, unemployment ticks up" The label the story was opened under. Audit finding 4's wrong word is hiring. | B | 3, the headline the stranger saw calls the payroll estimate a slowdown in hiring |
665:| `ap-september-jobs` | "average 68,000 jobs a month so far this year" "9,700 average new jobs created every month in 2025" Audit finding 5's baseline. The Associated Press states both averages. | B | 5, this year's average monthly gain is about 68,000, against about 9,700 a month in 2025 |
671:| `reuters-september-jobs-preview` | "Nonfarm payrolls likely increased by 90,000 last month" Published before 8:30 a.m. Eastern. The range in the same preview is 35,000 to 180,000. | B | 5, the surveys published beforehand had looked for about 90,000 |
