data/sources/coverage/cbs-july-decision.txt:93:The Federal Reserve said on Wednesday that it is leaving its benchmark interest rate unchanged, a sign policymakers expect inflation to ebb despite higher energy costs due to the
data/sources/coverage/cbs-july-decision.txt:97:Yet while the Fed voted to stand pat, three of the Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC)'s 12 voting members dissented from the decision. Such disagreement on the rate-setting panel is unusual, suggesting that some Fed officials are inclined to act now to extinguish inflation, according to Wall Street analysts.
data/sources/coverage/cbs-july-decision.txt:99:The dissenting members were Beth Hammack, CEO of the Federal Reserve Bank of Cleveland; Neel Kashkari, CEO of the Federal Reserve Bank of Minneapolis; and Lorie K. Logan, CEO of the Federal Reserve Bank of Dallas.
data/sources/coverage/cbs-july-decision.txt:103:In its policy
data/sources/coverage/cbs-july-decision.txt:106:"Inflation remains elevated relative to the Committee's 2% goal, in part reflecting supply shocks that have driven price increases in certain sectors, including energy," the FOMC said in its policy statement.
data/sources/coverage/cbs-july-decision.txt:107:The move to hold the federal funds rate — what banks charge each other for overnight loans — was widely expected by economists and investors after June inflation data showed consumer prices easing.
data/sources/coverage/cbs-july-decision.txt:110:During the press conference, Warsh reiterated the FOMC's commitment to stabilizing prices, saying that Fed officials "will not hesitate to act" to curb inflation. At the same time, he did not offer any hints on the future direction of monetary policy.
data/sources/coverage/cbs-july-decision.txt:115:Analysts said investors initially were relieved that the Fed had opted against hiking rates. But that sentiment wavered after Warsh's press conference offered few hints on the path of monetary policy.
data/sources/coverage/cbs-july-decision.txt:145:Federal Reserve
