data/sources/coverage/kpmg-august-jobs.txt:5:Payroll employment surged 162,000 in August, after being revised up by 55,000 for the previous two months. The public sector added back a net 35,000 jobs, 50,000 of which showed up in local government. The drop in local education was largely reversed. The federal government shed another 5,000 jobs. Employment in federal government has shed 48,000 jobs since the start of the year and is current hovering near 1966 lows.
data/sources/coverage/kpmg-august-jobs.txt:6:Private sector job gains were driven by leisure and hospitality, which added 62,000 after shedding 75,000 jobs over the last two months. That suggests that the layoffs due to the loss of the Temporary Protected Status (TPS) of Haitian workers contributed to earlier layoffs, which employers scrambled to replace in August.
data/sources/coverage/kpmg-august-jobs.txt:9:Employers in the Federal Reserve's Beige Book survey commented that filling low-wage jobs was getting more difficult. The National Federation for Independent Business reported increased labor shortages and difficulty finding workers.
data/sources/coverage/kpmg-august-jobs.txt:11:Manufacturing added 16,000 jobs with strong gains in metals and machinery. Those are protected by tariffs and enhanced by data center construction. Motor vehicle and parts production shed 4,500 jobs during the month. Vehicle sales surged in August as affluent consumers bought ahead of feared increases in tariffs. That is the exact behavior the Federal Reserve is tasked to avert.
data/sources/coverage/kpmg-august-jobs.txt:16:Separately, the unemployment rate held steady at 4.1% in August, for the right reasons. The participation rate rebounded after losing ground consistently over the course of the year, another sign that labor market conditions are improving. It rose to 61.6% from 61.4%. That is the highest rate since May. The gains were driven by 16 to 24 year-olds, who had been sidelined earlier in the year. That could reflect seasonal adjustment due to the return to school but is still encouraging.
data/sources/coverage/kpmg-august-jobs.txt:19:The new graduate unemployment rate reached 5.7% in June; it comes out with a lag. It is calculated by the New York Federal Reserve Bank but will likely fall once we get the actual data for August, although the jury is still out. The spread between the overall unemployment rate and that for new grads widened to levels more consistent with the early 2010s than six years into an expansion.
data/sources/coverage/kpmg-august-jobs.txt:20:The overwhelming majority of new grads, 89%, blame AI for the lack of hiring. There is little evidence that AI is displacing them. New graduates tend to be the last hired in a low-hire, low-fire, labor market, which we have seen for much of the year.
data/sources/coverage/newsweek-bessent-august-jobs.txt:4:After a recent report showed signs that the U.S. job market may be weakening, Bessent argued that fewer jobs are needed because deportations and lower levels of immigration have reduced the size of the workforce.
data/sources/coverage/newsweek-bessent-august-jobs.txt:7:“Secretary Bessent is right: President Trump’s immigration enforcement agenda is unburdening the U.S. economy while expanding opportunities for American citizens," White House Spokesman Kush Desai told Newsweek. "Wage growth in key sectors like construction and manufacturing reflects how this agenda is benefitting everyday American workers.”
data/sources/coverage/newsweek-bessent-august-jobs.txt:8:The administration's perspective was met with opposition from immigration advocates and economists Thursday. The American Immigration Council's director of research Nan Wu told Newsweek that the deportation campaign was focused on deporting workers the U.S. economy needs to keep vital industries afloat.
data/sources/coverage/newsweek-bessent-august-jobs.txt:13:Administration officials have argued that tougher immigration enforcement is helping ensure that available jobs go to American workers. But many economists view a shrinking labor force as a long-term challenge for economic growth because having fewer workers generally means that businesses can produce fewer goods and services.
data/sources/coverage/newsweek-bessent-august-jobs.txt:14:"Bessent is certainly right that the U.S. economy isn’t producing many jobs. Hiring is extraordinarily weak—hiring rates are where they were in 2011, in the aftermath of the great recession," Heidi Shierholz, president of the Economic Policy Institute, told Newsweek. "But the idea that weak job growth is somehow okay economically if it is the result of deportations is deeply wrong.
data/sources/coverage/newsweek-bessent-august-jobs.txt:15:"Deporting immigrants does not make U.S.-born workers better off. That is not how the economy works. Deportations don’t just affect those being deported, they ripple through the entire economy."
data/sources/coverage/newsweek-bessent-august-jobs.txt:16:Shierholz said that when there are fewer immigrant roofers and framers, fewer homes are built, meaning less work for U.S.-born electricians and plumbers.
data/sources/coverage/newsweek-bessent-august-jobs.txt:18:Trump administration agencies have acknowledged the importance of immigrant workers in certain sectors. In a recent federal rulemaking, the Department of Homeland Security stated that immigrants fill critical gaps across industries including agriculture, healthcare, construction and transportation, and noted that immigrants are a net positive for the U.S. economy overall.
data/sources/coverage/newsweek-bessent-august-jobs.txt:19:Newsweek previously reported that labor-market researchers and immigration analysts warned that mass deportations could reduce the number of available workers at a time when employers continue to report hiring challenges.
data/sources/coverage/newsweek-bessent-august-jobs.txt:20:Stuart Anderson of the National Foundation for American Policy told Newsweek before the 2024 election that without immigrants and their children, the U.S. would have seen little labor-force growth in recent years.
data/sources/coverage/newsweek-bessent-august-jobs.txt:24:During the campaign, Trump and Vice President JD Vance frequently argued that undocumented immigrants were competing with Americans for jobs and housing, and that tougher immigration enforcement would improve opportunities for U.S.-born workers.
data/sources/coverage/newsweek-bessent-august-jobs.txt:25:Bessent did not dispute that argument. Instead, he suggested that deportations and lower immigration have reduced the number of workers in the economy, meaning fewer new jobs are needed than in previous years.
data/sources/coverage/newsweek-bessent-august-jobs.txt:26:Economists remain divided over how immigration affects wages and employment. Some argue that lower immigration can reduce competition for certain jobs, while others contend that immigrants help drive economic growth by filling labor shortages, spending money and supporting new business creation.
data/sources/coverage/newsweek-bessent-august-jobs.txt:27:"The [administration's] policies give employers less incentive to invest in training or take more chances on marginal workers because there are fewer economic opportunities, in part because immigrants are also consumers, and reducing immigration also reduces the domestic market for goods and services," Anderson said.
data/sources/coverage/newsweek-bessent-august-jobs.txt:29:"Mass deportation could shrink our economy's capacity to grow by removing the entrepreneurs, taxpayers, and workers who help American businesses grow," Wu said. "The real solution needed here is building an immigration system that meets the country’s workforce needs and recognizes the vital role that immigrants play in generating wealth and prosperity."
data/sources/coverage/nbc-july-decision.txt:121:Federal Reserve votes to keep interest rates unchanged
data/sources/coverage/nbc-july-decision.txt:138:The Federal Reserve left its key interest rate unchanged Wednesday for the fifth consecutive meeting, despite lingering concerns about inflation that continues to pressure U.S. household finances.
data/sources/coverage/nbc-july-decision.txt:140:Nine members of the Federal Open Market Committee voted to keep rates steady at 3.5% to 3.75%. But three policymakers saw the case for raising interest rates and chose to vote against the decision to stand pat. It was the first time since 2016 that there were three dissents in the same direction over a policy change.
data/sources/coverage/nbc-july-decision.txt:142:In remarks following Wednesday’s announcement, Federal Reserve Chair Kevin Warsh reiterated the central bank’s commitment to its inflation goal.
data/sources/coverage/nbc-july-decision.txt:145:Immediately after the decision, the odds of a quarter-point rate hike at the Fed’s next policy meeting in September rose slightly, to 59%, according to the CME FedWatch tool.
data/sources/coverage/nbc-july-decision.txt:150:“Every month of above-target inflation has compounded the strain on Americans’ budgets,” Dallas Federal Reserve President Lorie Logan said in remarks this month, calling for rates to be “modestly” higher.
data/sources/coverage/nbc-july-decision.txt:161:Warsh, who took the reins of the central bank from Jerome Powell in May, has made it a hallmark of his tenure not to tip the Fed’s thinking about the path of monetary policy — much to the frustration of some market participants.
data/sources/coverage/nbc-july-decision.txt:162:“Chair Warsh’s communication void has encouraged other policymakers to speak more forcefully,” said Greg Daco, chief economist at EY-Parthenon, Ernst & Young LLP.
data/sources/coverage/nbc-july-decision.txt:164:“After months of upside inflation surprises, patience is wearing thin,” Daco wrote. “If inflation does not soon move back toward 2%, and remains elevated because of persistent supply shocks, stronger AI-related demand, tariffs, or the Middle East conflict, the case for additional policy firming will be clear.”
data/sources/coverage/nbc-july-decision.txt:170:Within days of their announcement, the latest tariffs were challenged in federal trade court, effectively throwing them into legal limbo even as importers prepare to start paying them.
