data/sources/coverage/nyt-august-jobs.txt:8:Employers added 162,000 jobs on a seasonally adjusted basis last month, the Labor Department reported on Friday, and the unemployment rate held steady at 4.1 percent. Job gains for June and July were revised upward by 55,000, bringing the average increase over the past three months to just over 70,000. And while the health care sector fueled much of the employment growth last year, last month’s gains touched even sectors that had appeared troubled, evidence of strength in the broader economy.
data/sources/coverage/nyt-august-jobs.txt:14:The tepid wage growth supports the Federal Reserve’s view that the labor market is not a source of inflationary pressure and reinforces its focus on stamping out inflation when Fed officials consider whether to raise rates later this month.
data/sources/coverage/nyt-august-jobs.txt:15:But it is still the case that job seekers, especially new entrants to the labor market and those who have been on the sidelines for a long time, are finding limited opportunities. Although layoffs are exceedingly low, the number of workers who have been unemployed for 27 weeks or more has swelled.
data/sources/coverage/nyt-august-jobs.txt:18:One of the more surprising drivers of growth last month was leisure and hospitality, which added 62,000 jobs after months of weakness as gas prices surged. Previous declines in the local government education sector, which may have been attributable to some extent to seasonal adjustment quirks, also partly reversed, with the sector adding 42,000 jobs.
data/sources/coverage/nyt-august-jobs.txt:27:Employers are also contending with an aging population and restrictive immigration policies. Hundreds of thousands of people from Haiti and other countries have also lost their ability to work legally after the Trump administration ended their temporary protected status.
data/sources/coverage/nbc-house-funding.txt:1:House passes short-term funding bill to prevent a shutdown before the election IE 11 is not supported. For an optimal experience visit our site on another browser. Skip to Content Politics U.S. News World Local New York Los Angeles Chicago Dallas-Fort Worth Philadelphia Washington, D.C. Boston Bay Area South Florida San Diego Connecticut Sports Business Health SHOPPING Tipline Subscribe Share & Save — Subscriber Hub Saved Newsletters Profile Subscription Preferences Search Sections U.S. News Politics World Business Sports Investigations Culture & Trends Health Science Tech Weather VIDEO Photos Sudoku NBC Selected NBC Asian America NBC BLK NBC Latino NBC OUT Local New York Los Angeles Chicago Dallas-Fort Worth Philadelphia Washington, D.C. Boston Bay Area South Florida San Diego Connecticut tv Today Nightly News Meet the Press Dateline Featured NBC News Now Nightly Films Stay Tuned Special Features Newsletters Podcasts Listen Now Business in America More From NBC NBC.COM NBCU Academy Peacock NEXT STEPS FOR VETS NBC News Site Map Help Follow NBC News news Alerts There are no new alerts at this time Search Facebook Twitter Email SMS Blue Sky Whatsapp Print Reddit Flipboard Pinterest Linkedin Latest Stories Politics U.S. News World Sports Business Health SHOPPING Tipline Government Shutdown House passes short-term funding bill to prevent a shutdown before the election The legislation, which already passed the Senate, now goes to President Donald Trump to become law, checking off the main item on Congress’ to-do list ahead of the midterms. Listen to this article with a free profile 00:00 00:00 The House voted to fund the government through Dec. 11. Chip Somodevilla / Getty Images file Share Add to Google Sept. 1, 2026, 2:06 PM EDT By Sahil Kapur WASHINGTON — The House voted Tuesday to pass a short-term funding bill and avert a government shutdown at the end of September, keeping money flowing at current levels until Dec. 11. The legislation, which has already passed the Senate , sailed through the chamber by a vote of 370-48. It now goes to President Donald Trump, who is expected to sign it. That would take the biggest legislative item off the to-do list for Congress ahead of the elections Nov. 3, punting stalled negotiations over a full-year funding deal until after the midterms. The two parties have clashed over the scope of federal spending as Democrats reject Trump’s call for a massive $1.5 trillion military budget, a spike from the already record-breaking $1 trillion that Congress gave the military this year. Funding the government requires 60 Senate votes to defeat a filibuster, meaning it must have bipartisan support even though Republicans control both chambers and the White House. House Appropriations Committee Chair Tom Cole, R-Okla., accused Democrats of blocking a deal to weaken politically vulnerable Sen. Susan Collins , R-Maine, who chairs the Senate Appropriations Committee. “I do want to finish all the bills before the end of the year. Now, whether we can do that or not is another matter,” Cole said, referring to the 12 appropriations bills that fund the government. “We won’t know if we’ll be doing that till after the election.” The winners of the midterm elections will feel empowered to press their demands in the lame-duck negotiations, knowing that they’ll have more power in the upcoming year. The election “has a tremendous impact,” Cole said. “We will act like we can come back and things will be exactly the way they were when we left. They won’t be. Everybody’s calculations will change. Everybody’s point of view will change. It’ll have an enormous impact about what happens for the balance of the year.” Rep. Rosa DeLauro, D-Conn., said she hopes to become chair of the Appropriations Committee again if Democrats win the House. But she said her only priority now is to finalize a full government funding deal for the new fiscal year before 2027 begins. “The midterm election will have an effect, but the goal is that we need to pass appropriations bills before the end of the year. That is a bipartisan view of where we need to go with the committee, and so that’s what we are going to press for,” DeLauro said. “Let’s get to the finish line.” Share Add to Google Sahil Kapur Sahil Kapur is a senior national political reporter for NBC News. Kyle Stewart contributed . About Contact Help Careers Ad Choices Privacy Policy Your Privacy Choices CA Notice Terms of Service NBC News Subscription Terms of Service NBC News Sitemap Closed Captioning Subscribe Advertise NBC Selected © 2026 NBCUniversal Media, LLC
data/sources/coverage/reuters-september-jobs-preview.txt:4:The anticipated moderation would follow a surprise jump in nonfarm payrolls in August, which economists partly attributed to volatility linked to the model the government uses to strip out seasonal fluctuations from the data. The Labor Department's Bureau of Labor Statistics will publish its closely watched employment report on Friday.
data/sources/coverage/reuters-september-jobs-preview.txt:6:"I am expecting a reaffirmation of the 'low-hire, low-fire' American labor market," said Joe Brusuelas, chief economist at RSM. "At this point, we're at full employment, and I think there will be nothing in the jobs report that will cause the Federal Reserve to change its bias from one more hike this year."
data/sources/coverage/reuters-september-jobs-preview.txt:8:"We suspect that the seasonally adjusted August nonfarm payroll print will be revised down as the BLS extends its seasonal adjustment procedure forward to incorporate the September data," said Marc Giannoni, chief US economist at Barclays. "The seasonal adjustment significantly exaggerated that month's employment gain. Had the August nonfarm payroll employment been adjusted with the August 2025 seasonal factors, it would have registered a drop of 74,000 jobs."
data/sources/coverage/reuters-september-jobs-preview.txt:11:Robust corporate profit growth and resilient domestic demand are for now shielding workers from layoffs. Historically low layoffs account for labor market stability, with hiring at a modest level.
data/sources/coverage/reuters-september-jobs-preview.txt:16:Economists also said it was unclear when the termination of Temporary Protected Status for hundreds of thousands of Haitian immigrants would show up in the data.
data/sources/coverage/reuters-september-jobs-preview.txt:18:Together with worker retirements, the Trump administration's immigration crackdown, which also includes deportations, has reduced labor supply, helping to keep a lid on the unemployment rate. The economy needs to create between 50,000 and 80,000 jobs per month to keep up with growth in the working-age population, economists estimated.
data/sources/coverage/reuters-september-jobs-preview.txt:21:Financial markets are pricing in a roughly 28% chance of further monetary policy tightening at the US central bank's October 27-28 meeting, down from about 69% a week ago, CME Group's FedWatch tool showed.
data/sources/coverage/upi-adp-august-jobs.txt:1:ADP: Private payrolls grew 38,000 in August, a decline from July - UPI.com U.S. News World News Korea Regional Review Latin America Region Odd News Entertainment Movies Music TV Sports NFL MLB NBA Soccer Voices Photos News US News World News Entertainment Sports Archive Almanac Trending Iran attacks Drugging trial Germany bombs Lake Ontario Hung Cao Cuba retail Venezuela oil Bobcat burglary Kyle Van Noy U.S. News Sept. 2, 2026 / 10:59 AM ADP: Private payrolls grew 38,000 in August, a decline from July By Joe Fisher Private payrolls rose by 38,000 in August, though it was less than expected and fell below the 46,000 jobs added in July, ADP reported on Wednesday. File Photo by Tasos Katopodis/UPI | License Photo Sept. 2 (UPI) -- Private payrolls rose by 38,000 in August, though it was less than expected and fell below the 46,000 jobs added in July, ADP reported on Wednesday. August's new hires report was the slowest month in terms of hiring since January with the manufacturing, professional services and information industries cutting jobs. "Pay can tell us a lot about today's choppy hiring," Dr. Nela Richardson, chief economist for ADP, said in a statement. "To understand hiring patterns, you have to look deeply into where pay growth is accelerating, where it's slowing, and for whom. Once-predictable wage growth has been overtaken by the complexities of demographic change, persistent inflation, and AI's effects on jobs." Manufacturing jobs posted the biggest losses, down 17,000, followed by professional and business services, down 16,000. The losses offset a 45,000 gain in education and health services jobs and 16,000 new hires in the leisure and hospitality industry. Large businesses of more than 500 employees added the most new hires, up 34,000, followed by small businesses with one to 19 employees, up 20,000. Small businesses with 20 to 49 employees decreased by 17,000 jobs. The Dow Jones consensus estimate projected 47,000 new hires in August, 9,000 jobs more than ADP's reported results. Wednesday's report comes ahead of the U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics' monthly nonfarm payrolls report expected on Friday. This week in Washington President Donald Trump signs an executive order to rename Lake Ontario as Lake America in the Oval Office of the White House on Thursday. Photo by Al Drago/UPI | License Photo Read More USS Abraham Lincoln docks in Thailand for shore leave BP names Ian Tyler as its third chair in less than a year U.N. climate report shows global temperatures rising too fast Topics Business Latest Headlines U.S. News // 42 minutes ago Federal judge: Google doesn't need to break up ad tech business Sept. 2 (UPI) -- A federal judge said Wednesday that Google does not need to break up its advertising technology business, rejecting a Justice Department request. U.S. News // 1 hour ago Poll: U.S. belief that government is corrupt hits 20-year-high Sept. 2 (UPI) -- Americans' belief in the corruption of government is at a 20-year high at 89%, according to a Gallup poll released Wednesday. U.S. News // 1 hour ago Patagonia sues Trump administration over Bears Ears National Monument Sept. 2 (UPI) -- Patagonia, an outdoor apparel company, sued the Trump administration for cuts dramatically shrinking the size of Bears Ears National Monument. U.S. News // 1 hour ago Woman charged with intimidating Lindsay Clancy jury by taking photos Sept. 2 (UPI) -- A woman arrested for taking pictures of jurors outside the courthouse where Lindsay Clancy is on trial was charged with intimidating jurors. U.S. News // 2 hours ago Doctors release vaccine recommendations for RSV, influenza, COVID-19 Sept. 2 (UPI) -- The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention usually launches a fall campaign to recommend vaccines for diseases like RSV and influenza. This year, however, the agency has not issued its updated recommendations. U.S. News // 3 hours ago Uber to lay off more than 3,000 people Sept. 2 