data/sources/coverage/upi-august-jobs.txt:1:Nonfarm payrolls grew by 162,000 in August, beat expectations - UPI.com U.S. News World News Korea Regional Review Latin America Region Odd News Entertainment Movies Music TV Sports NFL MLB NBA Soccer Voices Photos News US News World News Entertainment Sports Archive Almanac U.S. News Sept. 4, 2026 / 10:53 AM Nonfarm payrolls grew by 162,000 in August, beat expectations By Joe Fisher Chef Chris Wienhoff removes baked cod fish ready for customers at the first fish fry of the season at St. Gabriels Church in St. Louis on Feb. 24, 2023. Employment was up in food service, drinking establishments and local government education in August, the Bureau of Labor Statistics reported on Friday. File Photo by Bill Greenblatt/UPI | License Photo Sept. 4 (UPI) -- Nonfarm payrolls increased by 162,000 in August while the national unemployment rate remained firm at 4.1%, the Bureau of Labor Statistics reports on Friday. Total employment's job boost outpaced the 31,000 monthly job gain average over the past 12 months. August's report greatly outperformed the Dow Jones projection of 53,000 new payrolls. Employment was up in food service, drinking establishments and local government education while jobs decreased in the information industry. Food and drink service jobs rose by 59,000, compared to the 12,000 monthly average of new hires over the past year. Health care jobs continued to climb in August, though the gain was slower than the 12 month average, up 13,000 compared to the 32,000 average. Home health posted 11,000 new hires and hospitals added 8,000 jobs. Construction and other major industries like mining, quarrying, oil and gas extraction and wholesale trade changed little for the month of August. Jobs in construction increased by 22,000. Nonresidential specialty trade contractors added 8,000 workers, about 2,000 more than the monthly average over the past year. The average hourly earnings for all private nonfarm workers increased by an average of 10 cents, or about 0.3%, reaching $37.75. Over the past 12 months, average hourly wages have increased by 3.1%. The average workweek also increased in August, up about 0.1% to 34.4 hours in August. Overtime hours remained unchanged from July's 3.1 hours. The Bureau of Labor Statistics report painted a different picture from ADP's report on private payrolls on Wednesday. ADP reported that private payrolls increased by 38,000 in August, marking the slowest month of hiring this year. ADP's slow hiring figure was dragged down by 17,000 jobs lost in the manufacturing sector and 16,000 jobs lost in professional and business services. This week in Washington Vice President JD Vance briefs members of the media in the press room of the White House on Thursday. Photo by Annabelle Gordon/UPI | License Photo Latest Headlines U.S. News // 3 minutes ago Hawaii braces for Hurricane Lowell while still recovering from Lala Hawaii could face its second hurricane impact in a matter of weeks, as Hurricane Lowell threatens communities still recovering from Hurricane Lala. U.S. News // 1 hour ago Epinephrine injection recalled over leaking, cracked vials Sept. 4 (UPI) -- Drugmaker American Regent launched a voluntary recall of epinephrine injection due to leaking and cracked multidose vials USP 30 mg/ 30 mL, 1 mg/mL. U.S. News // 5 hours ago Judge officially declares mistrial in Lindsay Clancy case Sept. 4 (UPI) -- The judge in the Lindsay Clancy murder trial officially declared a mistrial Friday after the jury couldn't agree on a verdict. U.S. News // 2 hours ago North Carolina changes rules about mail-in ballot counting Sept. 4 (UPI) -- North Carolina's State Board of Elections changed the rules for mail-in ballots Friday hours before they were set to be sent out. U.S. News // 3 hours ago Trump: Trade cut with some nations if Fed doesn't lower rates Sept. 4 (UPI) -- President Trump called on the Fed to lower interest rates or warned he'll cut off all trade with countries with which the U.S. has a trade deficit. U.S. News // 4 hours ago New York police shoot and kill man wielding knife on Brooklyn Bridge Sept. 4 (UPI) -- New York City police shot and killed a man walking on beams of on the Brooklyn Bridge Friday morning after he threatened them with a knife. U.S. News // 4 hours ago Watchdog: Lori Chavez-DeRemer misused funds, created hostile workplace Sept. 4 (UPI) -- Former Labor Secretary Lori Chavez-DeRemer misused public funds, fostered a hostile work environment and carried out an inappropriate relationship while in President Donald Trump's Cabinet, a federal watchdog report said U.S. News // 5 hours ago FCC moves to dismiss ABC's free speech lawsuit Sept. 4 (UPI) -- The Federal Communications Commission asked a federal court to dismiss a lawsuit from ABC claiming the FCC was curbing its right to free speech. U.S. News // 6 hours ago Former Kentucky Gov. Matt Bevin sentenced to 60 days in jail Sept. 4 (UPI) -- Former Kentucky Gov. Matt Bevin was sentenced to 60 days in jail for his resistance to a court order charge Friday after his arrest in Bourbon County. U.S. News // 7 hours ago Kentucky hardens voter protections; USPS policy threatens mail vote Sept. 4 (UPI) -- Kentucky enacted new regulations to stop voters from being kicked off rolls and the USPS has quietly adopted a system to reject ballots en masse. Trending Stories Former Kentucky Gov. Matt Bevin sentenced to 60 days in jail FCC moves to dismiss ABC's free speech lawsuit U.N. to vote to adopt new, more accurate world map Ex-Christian podcaster charged with threatening online to kill Trump Volkswagen to cut 50,000 jobs as part of restructuring plan Follow Us Back to top About UPI Contact Corrections Advertisements Copyright © 2026 United Press International, Inc. All Rights Reserved. Terms of UsePrivacy Policy Privacy Manager
data/sources/coverage/yahoo-hassett-august-jobs.txt:1:Kevin Hassett says jobs numbers are down 'because we don't have this massive inflow of immigrants that are working'
data/sources/coverage/yahoo-hassett-august-jobs.txt:3:Some economists might find the latest jobs numbers underwhelming, especially compared to the eye-popping results from the early post-pandemic years — but Kevin Hassett, the director of the National Economic Council, went on CNBC (1)'s Squawk Box on Monday (2) to argue the numbers look worse than they are because the Trump administration has cracked down on immigrants, who previously propped up payroll reports.
data/sources/coverage/yahoo-hassett-august-jobs.txt:4:"The jobs numbers right now have been blockbuster," Hassett said. "The breakeven job number is a lot lower than it used to be because we've tightened the border. And so 120,000, 130,000 this year is sort of like 200,000 two or three years ago because we don't have this massive inflow of immigrants that are working."
data/sources/coverage/yahoo-hassett-august-jobs.txt:9:The April jobs report (3) had something for both sides of the argument. Job creation topped expectations for the month, with nonfarm payrolls up by a seasonally adjusted 115,000 for April, but average hourly earnings came in lower than expected — and the month also saw a noticeable drop in the labor force and tech-related jobs.
data/sources/coverage/yahoo-hassett-august-jobs.txt:11:Hassett's perspective on the jobs numbers is based on the breakeven, or the number of jobs the economy must add each month to keep the unemployment rate from drifting up. When immigration rises, the breakeven climbs. When it slows, the same payroll figure starts signaling a tighter labor market.
data/sources/coverage/yahoo-hassett-august-jobs.txt:12:For historical scale, a Federal Reserve staff note published last month (4) noted the breakeven averaged around 80,000 a month in the 2010s and ran as high as 185,000 in the 1970s, when baby boomers and women were flooding into the workforce.
data/sources/coverage/yahoo-hassett-august-jobs.txt:14:Some economists have offered partial support for Hassett's framing. Chicago Fed President Austan Goolsbee told (5) CNBC (6) on Friday the labor market has been "pretty much stable for a year, year and a half," calling it as stable "without being good." Dan North, senior economist for North America at Allianz, told CNBC the report was "fairly bulletproof this month."
data/sources/coverage/usatoday-missouri-house-map.txt:18:"The state’s Supreme Court, interpreting Missouri law, has ruled unanimously," the former U.S. Attorney General said. "This is not a matter in which the federal Supreme Court should be involved.”
data/sources/coverage/wkzo-reuters-oig-2026-09-08.txt:5:On Friday, a federal judge in Boston extended a ban stopping President Donald Trump’s administration from implementing a new USPS rule that would tighten mail-in voting requirements ahead of the November congressional elections.
data/sources/coverage/scotusblog-2026-09-14.txt:3:The Supreme Court on Monday declined to allow the U.S. Postal Service to carry out parts of a new rule on mail-in voting. In a brief, unsigned order, the justices left in place a ruling by a federal judge in Boston that bars the USPS from implementing aspects of the rule.
data/sources/coverage/scotusblog-2026-09-14.txt:7:As part of his administration’s broader effort to combat alleged voter fraud, President Donald Trump in March signed an executive order that directed federal agencies to take a series of steps on mail-in voting. For example, it required the Department of Homeland Security to send each state a list of the adult U.S. citizens in that state at least 60 days before a federal election and instructed the U.S. attorney general to investigate and prosecute state officials who allow people who are not eligible to vote to cast ballots.
data/sources/coverage/scotusblog-2026-09-14.txt:8:A group of 23 states, led by California, and the District of Columbia went to federal court in Massachusetts to challenge the government’s efforts to implement the executive order. They argued that the order clashes with provisions in the Constitution that give states the power to determine voter eligibility and to set the “Times, Places, and Manner” of holding congressional elections.
data/sources/coverage/scotusblog-2026-09-14.txt:9:U.S. District Judge Indira Talwani agreed. In an order on June 25, she temporarily prohibited the federal government from implementing parts of Trump’s executive order on or before the upcoming November elections in California, the states joining the lawsuit, or D.C.
data/sources/coverage/scotusblog-2026-09-14.txt:13:Sauer instead filed a new request with the Supreme Court, seeking to block Talwani’s preliminary injunction. He argued that Talwani’s “continuing prejudgment of the rule is baseless,” and her rationale is “simplistic,” and he rejected any suggestion “that the Postal Service is trying to federalize the rules for mail-in voting.” Instead, he wrote, “the Postal Service’s final rule imposes only modest envelope-design and addressee-information requirements for federal-election ballots sent via U.S. Mail.” Without a stay of Talwani’s order, Sauer continued, states will be able to mail out ballots that do not comply with the new rule.
data/sources/coverage/upi-miami-cargo-crash.txt:9:The Federal Aviation Administration said the aircraft departed from Luis Muñoz Marín International Airport in San Juan, Puerto Rico. The agency said it will investigate the incident.
data/sources/coverage/usatoday-scotus-missouri-house-map.txt:5:"A federal-election-administration disaster is unfolding in Missouri," Missouri Attorney General Catherine Hanaway, a Republican, said in an appeal filed late Sept. 4.
data/sources/coverage/usatoday-scotus-missouri-house-map.txt:10:Forcing Missouri to go back to the old map "renders a completed federal Primary Election utterly pointless − disenfranchising hundreds of thousands of voters who will now be moved into new districts and forced to vote for candidates they had no role in selecting," Hanaway wrote.
data/sources/coverage/usatoday-scotus-missouri-house-map.txt:11:"Looking forwards, the State is genuinely unsure whether it can switch its governing congressional map in time to run a timely federal election," she continued.
data/sources/coverage/usatoday-scotus-missouri-house-map.txt:18:Under state law, the ballot must be set by Sept. 8. Absentee ballots will be sent to military and oversees voters on Sept. 19 to comply with federal rules.
data/sources/coverage/thehill-trump-rates-august-jobs.txt:1:Trump threatens trade war if Federal Reserve rates aren't cut after strong jobs report Skip to content Toggle Menu Read The Paper Subscribe Account Profile Manage Newsletters Log Out Newsletters Morning Report 12:30 Report Evening Report Business Defense Healthcare Energy & Environment Technology Whole Hog Politics The Gavel The Movement Toggle Menu NEWS Senate House Administration Courts Media Campaign News 2026 Midterm Elections Education In The Know DC News State Watch Print Edition People in the News POLICY Defense Healthcare Energy & Environment Sports & Gaming Technology Transportation International Cybersecurity National Security Space BUSINESS Budget Taxes Personal Finance Lobbying HEALTH OPINION Columnists Congress Blog All Contributors Opinions – Campaign Opinions – Civil Rights Opinions – Criminal Justice Opinions – Cybersecurity Opinions – Education Opinions – Energy and Environment Opinions – Finance Opinions – Healthcare Opinions – Immigration Opinions – International Opinions – Judiciary Opinions – National Security Opinions – Technology Opinions – White House Submit Opinion Content EVENTS Upcoming Events About VIDEOS HILL INSIDER Subscribe Account Profile Manage Newsletters Log Out Newsletters NEWS POLICY BUSINESS HEALTH OPINION EVENTS VIDEOS HILL INSIDER Live: Clancy trial Trending: Fed interest rate John Fetterman Maria Bartiromo Sponsored: Content from Association for Consumer Debt Relief Just In US economy adds 162,000 jobs in August Business & Economy | 40 minutes ago Trump offers first comments on Lindsay Clancy case Administration | 46 minutes ago What to know about latest Missouri redistricting ruling State Watch | 50 minutes ago Federal judge raises ‘meritless re-prosecution’ in Lincoln Memorial Reflecting Pool hearing Court Battles | 52 minutes ago Lancaster County infant confirmed to have died from measles in Pennsylvania Healthcare | 58 minutes ago Trump says data center opponents face ‘poverty, crime and squalor’ Technology | 1 hour ago Live updates: August’s strong jobs numbers put rate cut in doubt; Trump signs beef deal Administration | 1 hour ago Supreme Court sides with GOP in fight over TV ad rates; Jackson dissents Court Battles | 1 hour ago View all Load more Business Trump threatens trade war if rates aren’t cut after strong jobs report Comments: by Julia Shapero - 09/04/26 12:04 PM ET Comments: Link copied by Julia Shapero - 09/04/26 12:04 PM ET Comments: Link copied NOW PLAYING President Trump called on the Federal Reserve to lower interest rates Friday after the August jobs report came in stronger than expected, threatening to cut off trade with several countries if the central bank doesn’t budge. The Bureau of Labor Statistics reported Friday that U.S. employers added 162,000 jobs last month, well above the 53,000 that economists were anticipating. Trump touted the “great” jobs numbers Friday before quickly turning his attention to the Fed, which he has long pushed to slash rates. “Lower the interest rates because the U.S.A. is a much stronger credit than it was just a short time ago!” he wrote in a post on Truth Social. “A STRONG COUNTRY MEANS A LOWER INTEREST RATE – IT’S A BETTER CREDIT.” “Without the United States agreeing to allow them their big surpluses, and we could stop that immediately, they would no longer be considered financially ELITE!” he added. “LOWER THE RATE OR I’LL STOP TRADING WITH COUNTRIES WITH WHICH WE HAVE A DEFICIT.” The Fed has held rates steady at a range of 3.5 to 3.75 percent at its last five meetings, as inflation has remained persistently above the central bank’s target rate of 2 percent. Annual inflation sat at 3.7 percent year over year in July, according to the personal consumption expenditures price index, the Fed’s preferred measure of inflation. However, Friday’s job numbers appear to increase the chances that the central bank raises rather than lowers rates. The latest data alleviates concerns that higher rates are weakening the labor market and allows the Fed to turn its attention to curtailing inflation. Traders are now pricing in a 60 percent chance that the Fed hikes rates by a quarter point at its next meeting this month, according to CME FedWatch, which tracks bets placed on future central bank decisions. Trump touted Fed Chair Kevin Warsh as a “great new leader” Friday even as he ramped up pressure on his pick to run the central bank. The president frequently feuded with Warsh’s predecessor, Jerome Powell, over rate cuts, threatening to fire him on several occasions. “The Fed Board, with its great new leader, must get smart – BE PATRIOTS for a change,” Trump said. “High interest rates put the U.S.A. at a very unfair disadvantage, and I won’t allow that to happen!” Add as preferred source on Google Tags Bureau of Labor Statistics Donald Trump federal reserve Interest rates Jerome Powell Jobs Report Kevin Warsh Kevin Warsh President Trump Truth social Copyright 2026 Nexstar Media Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. Comments: Link copied More Business News See All Administration Trump reveals Brazil, Argentina involved in controversial beef deal by Mallory Wilson 2 hours ago Administration / 2 hours ago Business Publix, Walmart, other grocers under investigation over beef prices by Nathaniel Rodriguez 3 hours ago Business / 3 hours ago Healthcare Epinephrine injection recalled over potentially fatal health risks by Zach Kaplan 4 hours ago Healthcare / 4 hours ago Business Diesel prices hit record high by Sarah Davis 8 hours ago Business / 8 hours ago See All Video/Hill.TV See all Hill.TV See all Video THE HILL TV Rising: September 4, 2026 by TheHill.com 09/04/26 12:27 PM ET THE HILL TV / 5 hours ago THE HILL TV Rising: September 3, 2026 by TheHill.com 1 day ago THE HILL TV / 1 day ago THE HILL TV Rising: September 2, 2026 by TheHill.com 2 days ago THE HILL TV / 2 days ago See all Hi