data/sources/coverage/wapo-venezuela-chevron-stub-2026-09-02.txt:1:Chevron makes its own deal to double Venezuelan oil production - The Washington Post Accessibility statementSkip to main content Democracy Dies in Darkness Democracy Dies in Darkness BusinessEconomyEconomic PolicyMediaPersonal FinanceFuture of WorkTechnologyBusiness of Climate BusinessEconomyEconomic PolicyMediaPersonal FinanceFuture of WorkTechnologyBusiness of Climate The 7 logo The 7 LIVE Chevron makes its own deal to double Venezuelan production The major oil company has plans to invest $7 billion as Trump aligns with its largest competitor in Venezuela. September 2, 2026 at 12:30 p.m. EDTYesterday at 12:30 p.m. EDT Make us preferred on Google (Patrick T. Fallon/AFP/Getty Images) By Evan Halper Chevron announced plans on Wednesday to double its crude production in Venezuela, as it competes with a giant new oil venture the Trump administration formed with a local competitor. The company said it had reached agreements in Venezuela to expand its footprint and within five years double production to 600,000 barrels per day, roughly the equivalent of half of all the oil currently exported from Venezuela. Chevron says it is investing $7 billion in the effort. Comments Sign up Company About The Post Newsroom Policies & Standards WP Creative Group Workforce Demographics Careers Media & Community Relations Accessibility Statement Sections Trending Politics Elections Opinions U.S. News World Style Sports Business Climate Well+Being D.C., Md., & Va. Obituaries Weather Arts & Entertainment Recipes Get The Post WP Intelligence Enterprise Subscriptions Manage Your Subscription Become a Subscriber Gift Subscriptions Mobile & Apps Newsletters & Alerts Washington Post Live Reprints & Permissions Post Store Books & E-Books Print Archives (Subscribers Only) Today’s Paper Public Notices Crosswords & Games Download on iOS Download on Android Contact Us Contact the Newsroom Contact Customer Care Contact the Opinions Te