## First read

**Story the page told me:** September payrolls rose by an estimated 29,000, too small a change to distinguish clearly from none; unemployment was 4.2 percent, and July and August payroll estimates were revised down.

**Questions left:** Do the revisions change the broader reading? What should I make of the rise in Black unemployment? What can this report tell the Fed before its next meeting?

**First lost thread:** “The Federal Reserve's two goals are maximum employment and price stability”. The piece shifts from explaining the two surveys to policy without carrying the reader across.

## The story

The [September BLS record](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-2026-09.txt:231) says “Both nonfarm payroll employment (+29,000) and the unemployment rate (4.2 percent) changed little in September” and “employment in July and August combined is 60,000 lower than previously reported.” That is the story the page opens with. Its account of what changed includes the revised August figure; its account of what remains open names preliminary payroll estimates and unpublished September hires. The page then overstates its rebuttal of the unemployment headline. That false sentence was filed as **Critical** in [r7, item 2](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r7-editor.md:32) and is still on this page. I do not file it again.

I found no later act that overtakes the October 2 report. The current [BLS archive](https://www.bls.gov/bls/news-release/empsit.htm) lists “September 2026 Employment Situation” first for 2026, and the [FOMC release list](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/2026-press-fomc.htm) begins with “9/16/2026”. The piece concerns a monthly release, prior revisions and scheduled next steps; the record does not establish an act, consequence and ensuing process that needs a separate dated sequence near the top. A [primary FactSet forecast](https://insight.factset.com/total-nonfarm-payrolls-for-september-2026-are-projected-to-rise-by-90000) also says “The median estimate for total nonfarm payroll employment for the month of September 2026 is 90,000”; it confirms a forecast reached through coverage, not a later result.

The six marked answers:

1. **What happened: yes.** The estimate, rate, private and government split, and combined revision are repeatable.
2. **Why it matters: partial.** The marked paragraph supplies the Fed action, meeting, inflation and pay figures. It leaves the reader to say what this official employment reading contributes to the pending decision. This is the policy connection already requested in earlier reviews.
3. **Easiest wrong reading: no.** The net jobs versus gross hires distinction is present, but the rebuttal of the rate uptick remains the previously filed Critical error. The threshold also asks readers to supply its conclusion themselves.
4. **Two surveys: yes.** The paragraph distinguishes payroll jobs from people and assigns each headline measure.
5. **What changed: yes.** Revised August, the prior pace and the longer comparisons are present, though the comparison sentence is crowded.
6. **What is unresolved: partial.** Preliminary status, hires and future dates are stated. The absence of an established cause for 29,000 remains the open answer 6 item already filed in [r7](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r7-editor.md:36).

## The stranger's read

For the **seven questions, in order**: what happened, act on nothing; why it matters, use the earlier request for a policy stakes sentence; the wrong reading, apply the r7 correction and make the threshold’s meaning explicit; the two surveys, leave, because the final sentence assigns both numbers; what changed, tighten the comparison in edit 3; what remains unresolved, retain the preliminary and hires statements while addressing the earlier cause item; who the national rate left out, leave the Black workers paragraph, which follows the group increase the [BLS release](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-2026-09.txt:246) singled out.

For **stalls and misreads, in order**: leave the headline’s unnamed “survey” because it gives the practical limit; connect 29,000 and 122,000 in the already requested catch correction rather than add another definition of significance; make that same correction distinguish the bureau’s wording from the statistical test; leave “406,000” as an expressly qualified estimate; leave “that tenth” because 4.1 to 4.2 precedes it and the next sentence gives the smaller move; leave the Black workers selection for the reason above; act on the three averages through edit 3; use the earlier policy stakes request for the PCE and Fed terms; leave the chart’s source attribution as provenance; leave “Seasonally adjusted” as a data label; leave “incomplete returns” because the sentence names the surveyed employers; leave the hires distinction because the quoted headline raises it; act on the long 78,000 sentence through edit 2; leave the unemployment range where it is, in the rate discussion before the jobs chart; cut the multiple payroll aside in edit 1; apply the r7 correction to “Neither half”; leave the first chart bar to the chart; name the August 5.2 million as hires in edit 4.

For **voice, in order**: leave the repeated headline figures across dek, panel and body because those are separate entry points; leave “Where this sits” as the chart’s orientation; apply the r7 correction so the USA Today quotation receives an accurate verdict; leave source history as provenance after the story; leave the November 3 date at both the first hires distinction and the unknowns section for readers entering either section; leave chronology’s repeated release because the table serves scanning.

