checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r2-editor.md:32:2. **Major · residual · TIGHTEN.** Page: “On September 16 the Federal Reserve raised the target range for the federal funds rate by 1/4 percentage point, in support of the Federal Reserve's dual mandate. The committee weighs maximum employment against price stability, and this is its last monthly employment reading before October 27-28. On September 30 the personal consumption expenditures price index for August increased 3.4 percent from a year earlier. The October employment report is scheduled for November 6.” Four dated facts leave answer 2’s point for the reader to assemble. Give the employment and inflation figures one sentence of purpose; remove the next report date here, since the chronology carries it.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r2-editor.md:40:6. **Minor · new · CUT.** Page: “On the Fed's 2026 meeting calendar, October 27-28 follows the September 15-16 meeting.” The dated policy passage has already placed that meeting; the following sentence states the unresolved decision.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r0-editor.md:13:I found no subsequent employment release or Fed policy decision through October 3. The [BLS release archive](https://www.bls.gov/bls/news-release/empsit.htm) still lists September 2026 as its latest Employment Situation. The page is not behind a new act. The sequence is September 16 rate increase, September 30 inflation release, October 2 jobs report, then the scheduled October 27–28 Fed meeting. No passage near the top gives that order with dates.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r0-editor.md:58:3. **Why does this report matter before the next Fed meeting?** The [Fed statement](</Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/fed-rate/fomc-statement-2026-09-16.txt>) says “The Committee decided to raise the target range for the federal funds rate by 1/4 percentage point to 3-3/4 to 4 percent”; the [calendar](</Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/fomc-calendar-2026-09-16.txt>) lists “October 27-28” after “September 15-16*”. This changes **answer 2** and displaces the release inventory in item 6.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r2-stranger.md:29:- (repeat, r1) "On the Fed's 2026 meeting calendar, October 27-28 follows the September 15-16 meeting." This repeats dates already given and answers no question.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-05-r9c-stranger.md:4:2. Why it matters: **Partly.** The Fed paragraph has the nearest line, "This report is the employment reading before that committee meets on October 27-28". "That committee" has no antecedent, and no sentence says what a jobs report tells an ordinary person.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r7c-stranger.md:1:**Held list:** I removed the "Since the previous story" block, the frame labels (including "Inspect the source data" and "Read the values and calculation"), the chart's bar-calculation sentence, the chronology column header, the St. Louis Fed wording, the October 27-28 and November 3 date phrasing, and the two forecast rows.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r7c-stranger.md:5:2. Why it matters: **Partly.** The page never says why a stranger should care about this report. The nearest text is "This is the last monthly jobs report before its committee meets on October 27-28".
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r1-stranger.md:32:- (repeat, r0) The same facts recur: 60,000 lower three times, Reuters 90,000 three times, and the October 27-28 meeting three times. "The Fed has decided nothing since September 16 and meets next on October 27-28" repeats the meeting calendar sentence just above it.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r1-redteam.md:1:1. **Major · new: “The Fed has decided nothing since September 16 and meets next on October 27-28.”** These are the [page bytes](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/dist/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000/index.html). A [September 30 Federal Reserve Board release](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/bcreg20260930a.htm) says the Board “finalized changes” through “two final rules.” The pinned [FOMC calendar](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/fomc-calendar-2026-09-16.txt) supports the October 27-28 meeting date, but only for that committee. The correct account is that the FOMC had made no further **rate decision**; the broader claim that the Fed had decided nothing is false.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r1-editor.md:13:The page has the right angle and opens with the result. It carries the revisions and the pending measures, but states one household estimate as an established increase and makes the policy setting read like a list of dates. One passage near the top does put September 16, September 30, October 2, and October 27–28 in order. As of October 3, the official listings show no later employment release or FOMC policy statement, so the jobs event has not overtaken the piece. [BLS release listing](https://www.bls.gov/ces/news.htm), [FOMC statement listing](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/2026-press-fomc.htm).
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r1-editor.md:32:3. **Major · residual · CLARIFY.** “On September 16 the Federal Reserve raised the target range for the federal funds rate by 1/4 percentage point. On September 30 the personal consumption expenditures price index for August increased 3.4 percent from a year earlier. On October 2 this report arrived. The committee meets next on October 27-28, and the October employment report is scheduled for November 6, so this is the last monthly jobs report before that meeting.” The patched passage orders the records but leaves their relevance, and the wage figure shown above it, for the reader to assemble. Ask for one stakes sentence while tightening this list.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r1-editor.md:42:8. **Major · new · FIX.** “The Fed has decided nothing since September 16 and meets next on October 27-28.” “Nothing” extends beyond rate policy. The [Federal Reserve’s October 2 release](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/orders20261002a.htm) says it “announced its approval of the application by Fleur Capital Corporation”. Confine the absence to an FOMC rate decision.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r7-stranger.md:5:2. Why it matters: **Partly.** The only answer is the Fed paragraph: "This is the last monthly jobs report before its committee meets on October 27-28". It never says why jobs data bears on rates.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r6-entailment.md:1:**Row 6, what-happened. Minor.** “The bureau's next monthly jobs report, for October, is scheduled for November 6, after the committee meets on October 27-28.” Checked `bls-empsit-2026-09` and `fomc-calendar-2026-09-16`. The release says the October report “is scheduled to be published” November 6. The calendar lists “October 27-28” but says each meeting date is tentative until confirmed. “Meets” states the future meeting as settled.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r4-stranger.md:4:2. Why it matters: **Partly.** The nearest sentence is "The bureau's next monthly jobs report, for October, is scheduled for November 6, after the committee meets on October 27-28." It never says what a jobs report tells the Fed or an ordinary reader.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r0-stranger.md:31:- (new) "The latest Federal Reserve policy decision among the records of this release is the September 16 statement, which does not decide the October 27-28 meeting." The "records" framing is self-referential, and the sentence is convoluted.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r3-stranger.md:6:2. Why it matters: **Partly.** The only answer is the held paragraph: "This report is its last monthly reading on employment before the committee meets on October 27-28." It never says what a jobs report tells the Fed or me.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-record-audit.md:27:6. **Major, grade B: the consequence and next decision are outside the chronology.** Page bytes: “Next Employment Situation, for October.” The [Federal Reserve’s September 16 statement](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20260916a.htm) says it raised its policy-rate range by “1/4 percentage point”; its [calendar](https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/fomccalendars.htm) places the next meeting on October 27–28. The [BEA’s August record](https://www.bea.gov/news/2026/personal-income-and-outlays-august-2026) gives 3.4 percent annual PCE inflation. The correct account is that this jobs release arrives between a rate hike and the next policy decision, while elevated inflation complicates the interpretation. The page should explain that stake without predicting the Fed’s decision.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-record-audit.md:66:The highest-value checks are the next two BLS revisions to September payrolls, November 3 JOLTS data for September hires and separations, the September price releases, and the October 27–28 Fed decision. The March 2026 [preliminary benchmark notice](https://www.bls.gov/news.release/prebmk.htm) also warns that a final benchmark is due later; its −79,000 estimate for **March** is not a September revision. These are future checks, not evidence of an outcome already known.

