checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r0-editor.md:44:8. **Minor · residual · CUT.** ““A Labor Department measure of gross hiring – before subtracting those who quit or lose their jobs – has been stuck in a rut for more than two years.” That hiring count, before quits and job losses are subtracted, is not the payroll change of 29,000.” This outlet detour uses more words than the distinction the reader needs; use the pinned JOLTS record for the unresolved September count. [Patch ledger](</Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/checks/working-notes/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.md:716>).
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r0-editor.md:50:11. **Major · residual · CLARIFY.** “The Job Openings and Labor Turnover release for September is scheduled for Tuesday, November 3, 2026, at 10:00 a.m. Eastern.” Say directly that the September gross hiring count is still unavailable. The ledger records the JOLTS admission, while the page leaves its implication unstated. [Patch ledger](</Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/checks/working-notes/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.md:716>).
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r0-editor.md:60:4. **Did gross hiring change in September? Unknown.** The [August JOLTS release](</Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-jolts-2026-08.txt>) says “Hires changed little at 5.2 million” and that the September release “is scheduled to be” published November 3. This changes **answer 6**. The page implies the unknown through a date but does not state it; a direct sentence displaces item 8.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r0-editor.md:62:The AP’s two-year gross hiring description was checked against the [BLS hires-rate series](https://www.bls.gov/charts/job-openings-and-labor-turnover/hire-seps-rates.htm), which shows 3.3% in August 2024 and 3.3% in August 2026. That primary series does not supply a September gross hiring result.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r0-redteam.md:1:**Minor · new: the two-year gross-hiring claim lacks its primary series.** The [built page](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/dist/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000/index.html) quotes “A Labor Department measure of gross hiring” as having “been stuck in a rut for more than two years.” The quotation matches the pinned [AP record](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/coverage/ap-september-jobs.txt). The pinned [August JOLTS release](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-jolts-2026-08.txt) says “Hires changed little at 5.2 million,” while the available, unpinned [BLS historical hires series via FRED](https://fred.stlouisfed.org/graph/fredgraph.csv?id=JTSHIL&cosd=2024-06-01&coed=2026-08-01) contains `2024-06-01,5137` and `2026-08-01,5192`. AP is accurately credited, but the pinned release alone does not let a reader check the two-year characterization.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r3-editor.md:32:1. **What does 29,000 measure, compared with hiring?** The [September BLS release](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-2026-09.txt:277) says “Total nonfarm payroll employment changed little in September (+29,000)”. The [JOLTS technical note](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-jolts-2026-08.txt:86) says “Hires include all additions to the payroll during the entire reference month” and calls the CES measure “the net employment change”. This sharpens answers **3 and 4** and displaces line edit **2**. Whether gross hiring itself slowed in September remains unknown; the page correctly says that count has not been published.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-record-audit.md:23:4. **Major, grade B: “hiring” is the wrong measured quantity.** Page bytes: “shows slowdown in hiring.” The September BLS release measures the **net change in payroll employment**. The latest available [BLS JOLTS record](https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/jolts_09292026.pdf) says August “Hires changed little at 5.2 million” and schedules September gross-hiring data for November 3. The correct account is that **net payroll growth slowed from August’s revised gain**; September gross hiring had not yet been reported. Use “payroll growth” in the headline, and distinguish hiring and separations when discussing the labor market’s movement.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-record-audit.md:25:5. **Major, grade B: the page gives no independent countercheck or adequate baseline for its slowdown reading.** Page bytes: “+29,000” and “Where the month sits.” The [BLS release’s private-sector row](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-2026-09.txt:393) ends “| 46 |,” meaning **+46,000 private payroll jobs**; government’s row ends “| -17 |.” [ADP’s separate payroll record](https://mediacenter.adp.com/2026-09-30-ADP-National-Employment-Report-Private-Sector-Employment-Increased-by-90,000-Jobs-in-September) says “Private employers added 90,000 jobs in September.” These are different surveys, not directly interchangeable results. The [pinned PAYEMS series](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/PAYEMS-2026-10-03.csv) also yields about **9,700 jobs per month in 2025** against **68,000 in January through September 2026**. The correct account is a weak September within a year stronger than 2025, with a separate measure showing stronger September private growth. The threshold for payroll growth that holds unemployment steady is itself disputed: [Dallas Fed’s estimate](https://www.dallasfed.org/research/economics/2026/0331) approached zero in late 2025, but it is not a verified September 2026 threshold.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-record-audit.md:48:**Passage grades, grouped by the answer they can change:** A: BLS headline and July/August revisions, carried in the story. B: survey uncertainty, household balance, Black unemployment, private/government split, historical baseline, gross-hiring distinction, and policy context; these are hidden or absent as detailed above. C: U-6, prime-age participation, longer-term unemployment, wage arithmetic, forecasts, and detailed industry rows; where supplied, Just the facts or Show the work is sufficient. D: release logistics and repetitive coverage that change none of the seven answers. Table A-9’s multiple-jobholder passage would ordinarily be C, but its **incorrect unit** makes the accuracy error Major.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-record-audit.md:60:**Strongly supported interpretation:** The payroll side weakened relative to the revised August result and forecasts, but the month does not establish a broad employment contraction or a measured drop in gross hiring. The unrounded unemployment change was small, and a separate private payroll measure was stronger. [BLS technical note](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-2026-09.txt:706), [St. Louis Fed](https://www.stlouisfed.org/on-the-economy/2026/oct/flash-report-unemployment-rises-slightly-job-growth-slows-september), [ADP](https://mediacenter.adp.com/2026-09-30-ADP-National-Employment-Report-Private-Sector-Employment-Increased-by-90,000-Jobs-in-September).
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r7-redteam.md:5:**Correct account:** The BLS did report a rise in the rounded unemployment rate from 4.1 to 4.2 percent, while describing the change as small. The payroll figure measures net jobs, so it does not establish a slowdown in gross hiring.
